{"parentUuid":"27836049-3e5d-4039-9e69-340df64536d3","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a5de01b","slug":"lovely-wishing-lantern","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01LspaUKFfaX346GfypxYop7","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→=== 0704.0117v1.pdf ===\n     2→Lower ground state due to counter-rotating wave interaction in trapped ion system\n     3→T. Liu1 , K.L. Wang1,2 , and M. Feng3 ∗\n     4→\n     5→1\n     6→\n     7→arXiv:0704.0117v1 [quant-ph] 2 Apr 2007\n     8→\n     9→2\n    10→\n    11→The School of Science, Southwest University of Science and Technology, Mianyang 621010, China\n    12→The Department of Modern Physics, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China\n    13→3\n    14→State Key Laboratory of Magnetic Resonance and Atomic and Molecular Physics,\n    15→Wuhan Institute of Physics and Mathematics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Wuhan, 430071, China\n    16→(Dated: November 4, 2018)\n    17→We consider a single ion confined in a trap under radiation of two traveling waves of lasers. In\n    18→the strong-excitation regime and without the restriction of Lamb-Dicke limit, the Hamiltonian of\n    19→the system is similar to a driving Jaynes-Cummings model without rotating wave approximation\n    20→(RWA). The approach we developed enables us to present a complete eigensolutions, which makes\n    21→it available to compare with the solutions under the RWA. We find that, the ground state in our\n    22→non-RWA solution is energically lower than the counterpart under the RWA. If we have the ion in the\n    23→ground state, it is equivalent to a spin dependent force on the trapped ion. Discussion is made for\n    24→the difference between the solutions with and without the RWA, and for the relevant experimental\n    25→test, as well as for the possible application in quantum information processing.\n    26→PACS numbers: 32.80.Lg, 42.50.-p, 03.67.-a\n    27→\n    28→I.\n    29→\n    30→INTRODUCTION\n    31→\n    32→Ultracold ions trapped as a line are considered as a promising system for quantum information processing [1]. Since\n    33→the first quantum gate performed in the ion trap [2], there have been a series of experiments with trapped ions to\n    34→achieve nonclassical states [3], simple quantum algorithm [4], and quantum communication [5].\n    35→There have been also a number of proposals to employ trapped ions for quantum computing, most of which work\n    36→only in the weak excitation regime (WER), i.e., the Rabi frequency smaller than the trap frequency. While as bigger\n    37→Rabi frequency would lead to faster quantum gating, some proposals [6, 7, 8] have aimed to achieve operations in\n    38→the case of the Rabi frequency larger than the trap frequency, i.e., the so called strong excitation regime (SER). The\n    39→difference of the WER from the SER is mathematically reflected in the employment of the rotating wave approximation\n    40→(RWA), which averages out the fast oscillating terms in the interaction Hamiltonian. As the RWA is less valid with\n    41→the larger Rabi frequency, the treatment for the SER was complicated, imcomplete [9], and sometimes resorted to\n    42→numerics [10].\n    43→In addition, the Lamb-Dicke limit strongly restricts the application of the trapped ions due to technical challenge\n    44→and the slow quantum gating. We have noticed some ideas [11, 12] to remove the Lamb-Dicke limit in designing\n    45→quantum gates, which are achieved by using some complicated laser pulse sequences.\n    46→In the present work, we investigate, from another research angle, the system mentioned above in SER and in the\n    47→absence of the Lamb-Dicke limit. The main idea, based on an analytical approach we have developed, is to check the\n    48→eigenvectors and the eigenenergies of such a system, with which we hope to obtain new insight into the system for\n    49→more application. The main result in our work is a newly found ground state, energically lower than the ground state\n    50→calculated by standard Jaynes-Cummings model. We will also present the analytical forms of the eigenvectors and\n    51→the variance of the eigenenergies with respect to the parameters of the system, which might be used in understanding\n    52→\n    53→=== 0707.1221v1.pdf ===\n    54→Motional frequency shifts of trapped ions in the Lamb-Dicke regime\n    55→I. Lizuain∗ and J. G. Muga†\n    56→Departamento de Quı́mica-Fı́sica, Universidad del Paı́s Vasco, Apdo. 644, Bilbao, Spain\n    57→\n    58→J. Eschner‡\n    59→First order Doppler effects are usually ignored in laser driven trapped ions when the recoil frequency is much smaller than the trapping frequency (Lamb-Dicke regime). This means that the\n    60→central, carrier excitation band is supposed to be unaffected by vibronic transitions in which the\n    61→vibrational number changes. While this is strictly true in the Lamb-Dicke limit (infinitely tight\n    62→confinement), the vibronic transitions do play a role in the Lamb-Dicke regime. In this paper we\n    63→quantify the asymptotic behaviour of their effect with respect to the Lamb-Dicke parameter. In\n    64→particular, we give analytical expressions for the frequency shift, “pulling” or “pushing”, produced\n    65→in the carrier absorption band by the vibronic transitions both for Rabi and Ramsey schemes. This\n    66→shift is shown to be independent of the initial vibrational state.\n    67→PACS numbers: 03.75.Dg, 06.30.Ft, 39.20.+q, 42.50.Vk\n    68→\n    69→I.\n    70→\n    71→1\n    72→\n    73→INTRODUCTION\n    74→\n    75→η=0.0\n    76→η=0.4\n    77→\n    78→0.8\n    79→\n    80→There is currently much interest in laser cooled trapped\n    81→ions because of metrological applications as frequency\n    82→standards, high precision spectroscopy, or the prospects\n    83→of realizing quantum information processing [1]. The absorption spectrum of a harmonically trapped (two-level)\n    84→ion consists of a carrier band at the transition frequency\n    85→ω0 and first order Doppler effect generated sidebands,\n    86→equally spaced by the trap frequency ωT , see Fig. 1. The\n    87→excitation probability of a given sideband, and thus its\n    88→intensity, depends critically\n    89→so-called Lamb-Dicke\n    90→\u0002 2 on the\u0001 1/2\n    91→(LD) parameter η = h̄kL\n    92→/(2mωT ] , with kL being the\n    93→driving laser wave number. If the LD regime is assumed\n    94→(η ≪ 1), the intensity of the kth red or blue sideband\n    95→scales with η k [1, 2, 3], k = 1, 2, 3, ..., so the number of\n    96→visible sidebands diminishes by decreasing η. It is then\n    97→usually argued that in the LD regime the absorption at\n    98→the carrier frequency is free from first order Doppler effect\n    99→[3, 4, 5]. Of course this is only exact in the strict LambDicke limit, η = 0, and for high precision spectroscopy,\n   100→metrology, or quantum information applications, it is\n   101→important to quantify the effect of the sideband transitions in the carrier peak, in other words, the asympotic behaviour, as η ∼ 0, of the frequency shift of the\n   102→carrier peak contaminated by vibronic, also called sideband, transitions in which the vibrational state changes.1\n   103→The inverse effect, in which the sideband is shifted by\n   104→\n   105→=== 0707.3224v1.pdf ===\n   106→arXiv:0707.3224v1 [q-bio.GN] 21 Jul 2007\n   107→\n   108→Tight-binding modeling of charge migration in\n   109→DNA devices\n   110→G. Cuniberti1 , E. Maciá2 , A. Rodriguez3 , and R.A. Römer4\n   111→1\n   112→\n   113→Institute for Theoretical Physics, University of Regensburg, D-93040\n   114→Regensburg, Germany g.cuniberti@physik.uni-R.de\n   115→2\n   116→Departamento de Fisica de Materiales, Universidad Complutense de Madrid,\n   117→E-28040 Madrid, Spain emaciaba@fis.ucm.es\n   118→3\n   119→Dpto. Matemática Aplicada y Estadı́stica, EUIT. Aeronáutics U.P.M., PZA\n   120→Cardenal Cisneros s/n, Madrid 28040, Spain antonio.rodriguezm@upm.es\n   121→4\n   122→Department of Physics and Centre for Scientific Computing, University of\n   123→Warwick, Coventry CV4 7AL, United Kingdom Rudolf.Roemer@warwick.ac.uk\n   124→\n   125→1\n   126→\n   127→Introduction and motivation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n   128→\n   129→2\n   130→\n   131→2\n   132→\n   133→The electronic structure of DNA . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n   134→\n   135→3\n   136→\n   137→3\n   138→\n   139→Numerical techniques for charge transport in the\n   140→quantum regime . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n   141→\n   142→4\n   143→\n   144→3.1\n   145→3.2\n   146→3.3\n   147→\n   148→Recursive Green function technique . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n   149→Transfer and transmission matrix approach . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n   150→Attaching leads . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n   151→\n   152→5\n   153→6\n   154→6\n   155→\n   156→\n   157→=== 0710.4345v1.pdf ===\n   158→arXiv:0710.4345v1 [gr-qc] 23 Oct 2007\n   159→\n   160→ULB-TH 95/02\n   161→UMH-MG 95/01\n   162→LPTHENS\n   163→January 1995\n   164→\n   165→A Primer for Black Hole Quantum Physics\n   166→R. Brout1 , S. Massar1,2\n   167→Service de Physique Théorique, Université Libre de Bruxelles,\n   168→Campus Plaine, C.P. 225, Bd du Triomphe, B-1050 Brussels, Belgium\n   169→R. Parentani3\n   170→Laboratoire de Physique Théorique de l’Ecole Normale Supérieure4\n   171→24 rue Lhomond 75.231 Paris CEDEX 05, France\n   172→Ph. Spindel5\n   173→Mécanique et Gravitation, Université de Mons-Hainaut, Faculté des Sciences,\n   174→15 avenue Maistriau, B-7000 Mons, Belgium\n   175→\n   176→1\n   177→\n   178→e-mail: smassar @ ulb.ac.be\n   179→Boursier IISN\n   180→3\n   181→e-mail: parenta@physique.ens.fr\n   182→4\n   183→Unité propre de recherche du C.N.R.S. associée à l’ Ecole Normale Supérieure et à\n   184→l’Université de Paris Sud.\n   185→5\n   186→e-mail: spindel @ umh.ac.be\n   187→2\n   188→\n   189→1\n   190→\n   191→\fAnd so long as we have this itch\n   192→for explanations, must we not\n   193→always carry around with us this\n   194→cumbersome but precious bag of\n   195→clues called History?\n   196→— G. Swift, Waterland (1983)\n   197→\n   198→2\n   199→\n   200→\fA Primer for Black Hole Quantum Physics\n   201→\n   202→Abstract: The mechanisms which give rise to Hawking radiation are\n   203→revealed by analyzing in detail pair production in the presence of horizons. In\n   204→preparation for the black hole problem, three preparatory problems are dwelt\n   205→with at length: pair production in an external electric field, thermalization\n   206→of a uniformly accelerated detector and accelerated mirrors. In the light of\n   207→these examples, the black hole evaporation problem is then presented.\n   208→\n   209→=== 0711.1406v1.pdf ===\n   210→Quantum Stochastic Heating of a Trapped Ion\n   211→L. Horvath, M. J. Collett, and H. J. Carmichael\n   212→Department of Physics, University of Auckland, Private Bag 92019, Auckland, New Zealand\n   213→h.carmichael@auckland.ac.nz\n   214→\n   215→R. Fisher\n   216→Department of Chemistry, Technical University of Munich, Lichtenbergstrasse 4, 85747 Garching, Germany\n   217→\n   218→arXiv:0711.1406v1 [quant-ph] 9 Nov 2007\n   219→\n   220→robert.fisher@ch.tum.de\n   221→\n   222→Abstract: The resonant heating of a harmonically trapped ion by a standing-wave light field\n   223→is described as a quantum stochastic process combining a coherent Schrödinger evolution\n   224→with Bohr-Einstein quantum jumps. Quantum and semi-quantum treatments are compared.\n   225→c 2007 Optical Society of America\n   226→OCIS codes: (270.2500) Fluctuations, relaxations, and noise; (270.1670) Coherent optical effects.\n   227→\n   228→1. Introduction\n   229→Trapped ions have featured prominently in the study of elementary quantum systems over a period of\n   230→many years. Some of the earliest experiments demonstrated the phenomenon of electron shelving or\n   231→quantum jumps [1-3]. Subsequently, with improved methods of laser cooling [4-6], and through resolvedsideband Raman cooling [7,8] in particular, it became possible to prepare an ion in the ground state of a\n   232→harmonic trap. Thus, a broad range of elementary quantum physics was opened up to experimental study\n   233→[9], including the spectroscopy [10-12] and photon statistics [13-15] of single-atom resonance fluorescence;\n   234→quantized Rabi oscillation and engineered quantum states of motion [16-18] (through a “phonon” analogy\n   235→of the Jaynes-Cummings model); and quantum decoherence, specifically, of superpositions of motional\n   236→states [19,20]. Today, trapped ion systems continue to be developed as potential building blocks of\n   237→quantum information processors [21-23].\n   238→Theoretical work on the quantized motion of trapped ions has mostly considered small displacements\n   239→from equilibrium—the so-called Lamb-Dicke regime [24]. It is common for the ion to be manipulated in\n   240→a standing-wave optical potential [25,26], in which case the potential is expanded to lowest order in the\n   241→Lamb-Dicke parameter\n   242→r\n   243→h̄\n   244→2π\n   245→η=\n   246→,\n   247→(1)\n   248→λ\n   249→2mωT\n   250→p\n   251→where η is the ratio of the ion ground-state position uncertainty ∆x =\n   252→h̄/2mωT and the wavelength\n   253→of the standing wave λ (multiplied by 2π); m is the ion mass and ωT the frequency. With the restriction\n   254→to the Lamb-Dicke regime, in many situations only one, two, or possibly a few quanta of excitation are\n   255→considered [16,17,27,28].\n   256→The assumption η ≪ 1 is a feature of the semiclassical treatment of atomic motion in laser light as\n   257→well, where it underlies the diffusion or Fokker-Planck equation models [4,29-31]. These models assume\n   258→that significant change to the particle (ion) momentum is built up from very many individual momentum\n   259→“kicks” (each of size h̄k = h/λ) with the wavepacket well-localized on the scale of λ; thus, in a harmonic\n   260→\n   261→=== 0809.1063v1.pdf ===\n   262→Target Location by DNA-Binding Proteins:\n   263→Effects of Roadblocks and DNA Looping\n   264→Gene-Wei Li*, Otto G. Berg†, and Johan Elf‡§\n   265→*Department of Physics and Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Harvard University, MA 02138 USA\n   266→†Department of Molecular Evolution, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden\n   267→\n   268→‡Department of Cell and Molecular Biology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden\n   269→§To whom correspondence should be addressed at: johan.elf@icm.uu.se\n   270→\n   271→The model of facilitated diffusion describes how DNA-binding proteins, such as\n   272→transcription factors (TFs), find their chromosomal targets by combining 3D diffusion\n   273→through the cytoplasm and 1D sliding along nonspecific DNA sequences. The redundant\n   274→1D diffusion near the specific binding site extends the target size and facilitates target\n   275→location. While this model successfully predicts the kinetics measured in test tubes, it has not\n   276→been extended to account for the highly crowded environment in living cells. Here, we\n   277→investigate the effect of other DNA-binding proteins that partially occupy the bacterial\n   278→chromosome. We show how they would slow down the search process, mainly through\n   279→restricted sliding near the target. This implies that increasing the overall DNA-binding\n   280→protein concentration would have a marginal effect in reducing the search time, because any\n   281→additional proteins would restrict the search process for each other. While the presence of\n   282→other proteins prevents sliding from the flanking DNA, DNA looping provides an\n   283→alternative path to transfer from neighboring sites to the target efficiently. We propose that,\n   284→when looping is faster than the initial search process, the auxiliary binding sites further\n   285→extend the effective target region and therefore facilitate the target location.\n   286→\n   287→Introduction\n   288→\n   289→DNA-binding proteins control both when and how the genome functions. Moreover, they limit how fast\n   290→the genomic information can be accessed. For instance, the rate at which a transcription factor (TF) finds its\n   291→specific binding site determines how rapidly the corresponding gene can be turned on or off. When this rate is\n   292→low compared to the speed of the regulated process, the binding and unbinding kinetics of the protein may be\n   293→more important for the overall system behavior than its often well-characterized equilibrium properties.\n   294→Although seemingly a simple process, target location by DNA-binding proteins requires scanning through\n   295→millions to billions of similar nonspecific DNA sequences. It was hypothesized [1, 2] and later demonstrated [3]\n   296→that DNA-binding proteins partly diffuse (slide) along the nonspecific DNA while scanning for the specific site\n   297→(Fig. 1). The process of alternating 3D diffusion in cytoplasm and 1D diffusion along DNA is called facilitated\n   298→target location [4]. Originally discovered in vitro, this mechanism facilitates the process of target location by\n   299→extending the target size via efficient sliding from neighboring nonspecific DNA, while taking advantage of\n   300→three-dimensional diffusion to carry the molecule over large distances. The complexity of this search process\n   301→has attracted both theoretical and experimental attention over the past few decades [2, 5-14].\n   302→In a living cell, however, the target location is further complicated by the vast amount of other proteins,\n   303→both cytoplasmic and DNA-bound. More than 50% of the dry weight of an E. coli cell is protein [15]. The 3D\n   304→diffusion in the cytoplasm is slowed down by at least one order of magnitude compared to that in water.\n   305→Furthermore, more than 100,000 proteins are associated with DNA per cell, mostly bound nonspecifically [16,\n   306→17]. As a result, the bacterial chromosome is at least 20-30% occupied, with mostly uniformly distributed short\n   307→naked DNA fragments [18]. The sliding motion along DNA must be hindered as well. Therefore, for facilitated\n   308→target location, it is important to consider the effects of these macromolecules that are absent in the in vitro\n   309→settings.\n   310→In the current paper we adapt the theory for facilitated target location to the in vivo situation, with\n   311→emphasis on the crowding on chromosomal DNA in bacterial cells. First, the high occupancy by other proteins\n   312→\n   313→=== 0810.0078v2.pdf ===\n   314→Regular black hole in three dimensions\n   315→Yun Soo Myung∗\n   316→Institute of Basic Science and School of Computer Aided Science,\n   317→Inje University, Gimhae 621-749, Korea\n   318→\n   319→arXiv:0810.0078v2 [gr-qc] 9 Apr 2009\n   320→\n   321→Myungseok Yoon†\n   322→Center for Quantum Spacetime, Sogang University, Seoul 121-742, Korea\n   323→(Dated: November 4, 2018)\n   324→\n   325→Abstract\n   326→We find a new black hole in three dimensional anti-de Sitter space by introducing an anisotropic\n   327→perfect fluid inspired by the noncommutative black hole. This is a regular black hole with two\n   328→horizons. We compare thermodynamics of this black hole with that of non-rotating BTZ black\n   329→hole. The first-law of thermodynamics is not compatible with the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy.\n   330→PACS numbers: 04.70.Dy,97.60.Lf\n   331→Keywords: Regular black hole, black hole thermodynamics\n   332→\n   333→∗\n   334→\n   335→Electronic address: ysmyung@inje.ac.kr\n   336→\n   337→†\n   338→\n   339→Electronic address: younms@sogang.ac.kr\n   340→\n   341→1\n   342→\n   343→\fI.\n   344→\n   345→INTRODUCTION\n   346→\n   347→Hawking’s semiclassical analysis of the black hole radiation suggests that most information about initial states is shielded behind the event horizon and will not back to the\n   348→asymptotic region far from the evaporating black hole [1]. This means that the unitarity is\n   349→violated by an evaporating black hole. However, this conclusion has been debated by many\n   350→authors for three decades [2, 3, 4]. It is closely related to a long standing puzzle of the information loss paradox, which states the question of whether the formation and subsequent\n   351→evaporation of a black hole is unitary. One of the most urgent problems in black hole physics\n   352→is to resolve the unitarity issue. In this direction, a complete description of black hole evaporation is an important issue. In order to determine the final state of evaporation process,\n   353→a more precise treatment including quantum gravity effects and backreaction is generally\n   354→required. At present, two leading candidates for quantum gravity are the string theory and\n   355→the loop quantum gravity. Interestingly, the semiclassical analysis of the loop quantum black\n   356→hole provides a regular black hole (RBH) without singularity whose minimum size rc is at\n   357→Planck scale lp , in contrast to the classical one [5].\n   358→RBHs have been considered, dating back to Bardeen [6], for avoiding the curvature singularity beyond the event horizon in black hole physics [7]. Their causal structures are\n   359→similar to the Reissner-Nordström black hole with the singularity replaced by de Sitter\n   360→p\n   361→space-time with curvature radius r̃0 = 3/Λ [8, 9]. Hayward has discussed the formation\n   362→and evaporation process of a RBH with minimum size l [10] which can be identified with\n   363→the minimal length induced from the string theory [11], and its thermodynamic analysis was\n   364→\n   365→=== 0901.0603v2.pdf ===\n   366→Black hole thermodynamics with generalized uncertainty principle\n   367→Li Xiang∗ and X. Q. Wen\n   368→\n   369→arXiv:0901.0603v2 [gr-qc] 18 Mar 2009\n   370→\n   371→Center for Relativistic Astrophysics and High Energy Physics,\n   372→Department of Physics, Nanchang University,\n   373→Nanchang, 330031, Jiangxi province, P. R. China\n   374→\n   375→Abstract\n   376→In the standard viewpoint, the temperature of a stationary black hole is proportional\n   377→to its surface gravity, TH = h̄κ/2π. This is a semiclassical result and the quantum gravity\n   378→effects are not taken into consideration. This Letter explores a unified expression for the black\n   379→hole temperature in the sense of a generalized uncertainty principle(GUP). Our discussion\n   380→involves a heuristic analysis of a particle which is absorbed by the black hole. Besides a class\n   381→of static and spherically symmetric black holes, an axially symmetric Kerr-Newman black\n   382→hole is considered. Different from the existing literature, we suggest that the black hole’s\n   383→irreducible mass represent the characteristic size in the absorption process. The information\n   384→capacity of a remnant is also discussed by Bousso’s D-bound in de Sitter spacetime.\n   385→Keywords: generalized uncertainty principle, black hole temperature, irreducible mass,\n   386→remnant.\n   387→PACS numbers: 04.70.Dy, 04.70.-s\n   388→\n   389→∗\n   390→\n   391→xiang.lee@163.com\n   392→\n   393→1\n   394→\n   395→\f1\n   396→\n   397→Introduction\n   398→\n   399→Heisenberg’s uncertainty relation is one of the fundamental principles of quantum mechanics.\n   400→This principle only meets the quantum effects of matters, and it does not directly describe\n   401→the quantum fluctuations of spacetimes. However, many efforts have shown that Heisenberg’s\n   402→principle may suffer a modification[1]-[10], in the context of quantum gravity. Concretely, a\n   403→generalized uncertainty principle(GUP) reads\n   404→∆x ≥\n   405→\n   406→α\n   407→h̄\n   408→+ ∆p,\n   409→∆p h̄\n   410→\n   411→(1)\n   412→\n   413→where α ∼ G. The second term on the r.h.s means a new duality, which is firstly related to\n   414→the spacetime uncertainty principle[1, 3] and the scattering amplitude of high energy string[2].\n   415→This term is also attributed to gravity in some gedanken experiments[4, 5, 7, 9]. Different from\n   416→\n   417→=== 0905.4129v3.pdf ===\n   418→arXiv:0905.4129v3 [math-ph] 7 Jul 2009\n   419→\n   420→Perturbations of the Kerr black hole in the\n   421→class of axisymmetric artificial black holes.\n   422→G.Eskin, Department of Mathematics, UCLA,\n   423→Los Angeles, CA 90095-1555, USA. E-mail: eskin@math.ucla.edu\n   424→November 20, 2018\n   425→Abstract\n   426→Artificial black holes (called also acoustic or optical black holes)\n   427→are the black holes for the linear wave equation describing the wave\n   428→propagation in a moving medium. They attracted a considerable interest of physicists who study them to better understand the black\n   429→holes in general relativity. We consider the case of stationary axisymmetric metrics and we show that the Kerr black hole is not stable\n   430→under perturbations in the class of all axisymmetric metrics. We describe families of axisymmetric metrics having black holes that are\n   431→the perturbations of the Kerr black hole. We also show that the ergosphere can be determined by boundary measurements and we prove\n   432→the uniform boundness of the solution in the exterior of the black hole\n   433→when the event horizon coincides with the ergosphere.\n   434→\n   435→1\n   436→\n   437→Introduction.\n   438→\n   439→Consider the wave equation:\n   440→\n   441→(1.1)\n   442→\n   443→n\n   444→X\n   445→\n   446→∂\n   447→p\n   448→n\n   449→(−1) g(x) ∂xj\n   450→j,k=0\n   451→1\n   452→\n   453→\u0012\n   454→p\n   455→\n   456→∂u(x0 , x)\n   457→(−1)n g(x)g (x)\n   458→∂xk\n   459→jk\n   460→\n   461→\u0013\n   462→\n   463→=0\n   464→\n   465→in Ω × R, where Ω is a smooth bounded domain in Rn , x0 ∈ R is the time\n   466→variable, g(x) = det[gjk (x)]nj,k=0, [gjk (x)]nj,k=0 = ([g jk (x)]nj,k=0)−1 is a pseudoRiemanian metric with the Lorentz signature (1, −1, ..., −1). We assume that\n   467→1\n   468→\n   469→=== 0907.2248v2.pdf ===\n   470→Instability and new phases of higher-dimensional rotating black holes\n   471→Óscar J. C. Diasa , Pau Figuerasb , Ricardo Monteiroa , Jorge E. Santosa , Roberto Emparanc ∗\n   472→a\n   473→\n   474→DAMTP, Centre for Mathematical Sciences,\n   475→University of Cambridge, Wilberforce Road,\n   476→Cambridge CB3 0WA, UK\n   477→\n   478→b\n   479→\n   480→Centre for Particle Theory & Department of Mathematical Sciences,\n   481→Science Laboratories, South Road,\n   482→Durham DH1 3LE, UK\n   483→\n   484→arXiv:0907.2248v2 [hep-th] 23 Oct 2009\n   485→\n   486→c\n   487→\n   488→Institució Catalana de Recerca i Estudis Avançats (ICREA),\n   489→Passeig Lluı́s Companys 23,\n   490→E-08010 Barcelona, Spain, and\n   491→Departament de Fı́sica Fonamental,\n   492→Universitat de Barcelona, Marti i Franquès 1,\n   493→E-08028 Barcelona, Spain\n   494→\n   495→It has been conjectured that higher-dimensional rotating black holes become unstable at a sufficiently large value of the rotation, and that new black holes with pinched horizons appear at the\n   496→threshold of the instability. We search numerically, and find, the stationary axisymmetric perturbations of Myers-Perry black holes with a single spin that mark the onset of the instability and\n   497→the appearance of the new black hole phases. We also find new ultraspinning Gregory-Laflamme\n   498→instabilities of rotating black strings and branes.\n   499→\n   500→Black holes are the most basic and fascinating objects\n   501→in General Relativity and the study of their properties\n   502→is essential for a better understanding of the dynamics\n   503→of spacetime at its most extreme. In higher-dimensional\n   504→spacetimes a vast landscape of novel black holes has begun to be uncovered [1]. Its layout — i.e., the connections between different classes of black holes in the space\n   505→of solutions — hinges crucially on the analysis of their\n   506→classical stability: most novel black hole phases are conjectured to branch-off at the threshold of an instability\n   507→of a known phase. Showing how this happens is an outstanding open problem that we address in this paper.\n   508→The best known class of higher-dimensional black\n   509→holes, discovered by Myers and Perry (MP) in [2], appear in many respects as natural generalizations of the\n   510→Kerr solution. In particular, their horizon is topologically spherical. However, the actual shape of the horizon\n   511→can differ markedly from the four-dimensional one, which\n   512→is always approximately round with a radius parametrically ∼ GM . This is not so in d ≥ 6. Considering for\n   513→simplicity\n   514→\u0004\n   515→\u0005 the case where only one spin J is turned on (of\n   516→the d−1\n   517→independent angular momenta available), it is\n   518→2\n   519→possible to have black holes with arbitrarily large J for a\n   520→\n   521→=== 0907.4819v1.pdf ===\n   522→Presence of pKa Perturbations Among Homeodomain\n   523→Residues Facilitates DNA Binding\n   524→Christopher M. Frenz1* and Philippe P. Lefebvre1\n   525→1\n   526→Department of Otolaryngology\n   527→University of Liege\n   528→CHU de Liege\n   529→4000 Liege, Belgium\n   530→*\n   531→Corresponding Author: cfrenz@gmail.com\n   532→\n   533→Abstract - Homeodomain containing proteins are a broad\n   534→class of DNA binding proteins that are believed to primarily\n   535→function as transcription factors. Electrostatics interactions\n   536→have been demonstrated to be critical for the binding of the\n   537→homeodomain to DNA. An examination of the electrostatic\n   538→state of homeodomain residues involved in DNA phosphate\n   539→binding has demonstrated the conserved presence of upward\n   540→shifted pKa values among the basic residue of lysine and\n   541→arginine. It is believed that these pKa perturbation work to\n   542→facilitate binding to DNA since they ensure that the basic\n   543→residues always retain a positive charge.\n   544→Keywords: Computational Biology, Structural Biology,\n   545→Protein Electrostatics, Protein Evolution\n   546→\n   547→1\n   548→\n   549→Introduction\n   550→\n   551→Homeobox proteins are characterized by a 60 amino\n   552→acid helix-turn-helix motif that is involved in the binding of\n   553→DNA. Homeobox proteins have been characterized as\n   554→transcription factors that regulate gene expression during the\n   555→development and differentiation of neural systems within\n   556→mammals, although they are also present in a wide range of\n   557→eukaryotes and in bacteria as repressor proteins [1,2]. Recent\n   558→examinations of the forces involved in the binding of the\n   559→homeodomains of Antp, NK2, Engrailed, and Matα2 to DNA\n   560→have demonstrated that DNA binding is largely an\n   561→electrostatic phenomena.\n   562→Unbound homeodomains were\n   563→demonstrated to have chloride ions bound to the residues that\n   564→will coordinate with the phosphates of DNA upon binding.\n   565→During the binding process these chloride ions are displaced\n   566→along with calcium ions that were bound to the DNA\n   567→phosphates, and the ion interactions replaced by electrostatic\n   568→coordination between homeodomain residues and the DNA\n   569→phosphates [2].\n   570→Electrostatics playing a key role in ligand binding is\n   571→not limited to homeodomain containing proteins, however, and\n   572→\n   573→=== 1002.2759v1.pdf ===\n   574→Influence of pore dielectric boundaries on the translocation barrier of DNA\n   575→Stefan Kesselheim,1, ∗ Marcello Sega,2, † and Christian Holm1, ‡\n   576→1\n   577→\n   578→arXiv:1002.2759v1 [cond-mat.soft] 14 Feb 2010\n   579→\n   580→Institute of Computational Physics, University of Stuttgart,\n   581→Pfaffenwaldring 27, D-70569 Stuttgart, Germany\n   582→2\n   583→Department of Physics and INFN, University of Trento, via Sommarive 14, I-38123 Trento, Italy\n   584→(Dated: October 30, 2018)\n   585→We investigate the impact of dielectric boundary forces on the translocation process of charged\n   586→rigid DNA segments through solid neutral nanopores. We assess the electrostatic contribution to\n   587→the translocation free energy barrier of a model DNA segment by evaluating the potential of mean\n   588→force in absence and presence of polarization effects by means of coarse-grained molecular dynamics\n   589→simulations. The effect of induced polarization charges has been taken into account by employing\n   590→ICC⋆ , a recently developed algorithm that can efficiently compute induced polarization charges\n   591→induced on suitably discretized dielectric boundaries. Since water has a higher dielectric constant\n   592→than the pore walls, polarization effects repel charged objects in the vicinity of the interface, with\n   593→the effect of significantly increasing the free energy barrier. Another investigated side effect is the\n   594→change of the counterion distribution around the charged polymer in presence of the induced pore\n   595→charges. Furthermore we investigate the influence of adding salt to the solution.\n   596→PACS numbers:\n   597→\n   598→I.\n   599→\n   600→INTRODUCTION\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"05e096b4-9d4d-4956-9f1c-b20edc5a49fd","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:03:26.413Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"27836049-3e5d-4039-9e69-340df64536d3"}
