{"parentUuid":"b1d2d1e3-c934-42f2-8d58-bc488d50e9ff","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a5de01b","slug":"lovely-wishing-lantern","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_vrtx_019M9yPkDV4nzPJKVzURfVyV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01LdmjSj17NEpapQPsEGrLXk","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-root/2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_017qp5RfQsP2SwYRFpmmRwc6.txt","offset":600,"limit":600}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10042,"cache_read_input_tokens":131430,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10042,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXb5qsZqjmtVKxsg1zenE","type":"assistant","uuid":"1ce7f32e-a51c-4439-88d4-89a5fa4566a8","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:03:31.985Z"}
{"parentUuid":"1ce7f32e-a51c-4439-88d4-89a5fa4566a8","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a5de01b","slug":"lovely-wishing-lantern","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01LdmjSj17NEpapQPsEGrLXk","type":"tool_result","content":"   600→INTRODUCTION\n   601→\n   602→The problem of polymer translocation through\n   603→nanometer-sized pores has recently stimulated much experimental [1–9], theoretical [10–19] and simulation based\n   604→[20–26] research, due to their major role in biological processes and to potential technological applications. Molecular transport is indeed one of the key functions fulfilled\n   605→by the plasma and nuclear membranes of the cell, and\n   606→a sizable amount of transport mechanisms which work\n   607→in the cell are characterized by the same general design: namely, by the presence of pores, mostly through\n   608→membrane proteins. The controlled transport of single\n   609→molecules through synthetic or biological nanopores is\n   610→considered as a versatile tool of single molecule sensing\n   611→and to be a most promising candidate for rapid DNA\n   612→sequencing. A recent review reported more than 100 experimental approaches [27] that try to unveil how these\n   613→systems can be technologically used as a probe to the\n   614→world of single molecules. Researchers employed biological nanopores, or crafted synthetic ones[28], for different\n   615→technological and scientific purposes, often with an aim\n   616→towards DNA sequencing [29–31].\n   617→The complex interplay of interactions – electrostatic,\n   618→hydrodynamic and specific chemical ones – and the entropic properties of chain molecules makes a full understanding of these systems very difficult. In this article we\n   619→investigate a contribution to the translocation free energy\n   620→barrier of stiff DNA which has often been neglected so\n   621→far, namely the role of the dielectric mismatch between\n   622→solvent and pore. The presence of the interface between\n   623→\n   624→\n   625→=== 1011.5614v2.pdf ===\n   626→Parity-relevant Zitterbewegung and quantum simulation by a single trapped ion\n   627→Kunling Wang1,2 , Tao Liu3 , Mang Feng1∗ , Wanli Yang1 and Kelin Wang4\n   628→1\n   629→\n   630→arXiv:1011.5614v2 [quant-ph] 4 Jan 2011\n   631→\n   632→State Key Laboratory of Magnetic Resonance and Atomic and Molecular Physics,\n   633→Wuhan Institute of Physics and Mathematics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Wuhan, 430071, China\n   634→2\n   635→Graduate School of the Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China\n   636→3\n   637→The School of Science, Southwest University of Science and Technology, Mianyang 621010, China\n   638→4\n   639→The Department of Modern Physics, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China\n   640→Zitterbewegung (ZB), the trembling of free relativistic electrons in a vacuum could be simulated\n   641→by a single trapped ion. We focus on the variations of ZB under different parity conditions and find\n   642→no ZB in the case of odd or even parity. ZB occurs only for admixture of the odd and even parity\n   643→states. We also show the similar role played by the parity operator for the trapped ion in Fock-state\n   644→representation and the space inversion operator for a realistic relativistic electron. Although the ZB\n   645→effect is invisible in a relativistic electron, preparation of the trapped ion in different parity states is\n   646→a sophisticated job, which makes it possible to observe the parity relevant ZB effects with currently\n   647→available techniques.\n   648→PACS numbers: 31.30.J-, 42.50.Dv, 03.65.Pm\n   649→\n   650→Since the discovery by Schrödinger [1], Zitterbewegung\n   651→(ZB), i.e., the trembling of free relativistic electrons, has\n   652→drawn more and more attention and interests over past\n   653→years [2–8]. It has been generally believed that the trembling of a relativistic electron is resulted by the interference between negative and positive energy components,\n   654→a typical relativistic feature of the Dirac electron. Up\n   655→to now, however, no direct observation of ZB has been\n   656→achieved due to inaccessibility with current experimental techniques, which led to some questioning on the ZB\n   657→[6, 9]. Moreover, there have been alternative explanations\n   658→for the origin of the ZB, such as the continuously virtual transition process between different internal states\n   659→in view of quantum field theory [8] or the relevance to the\n   660→complex phase factor in context of space-time algebra [3].\n   661→By quantum simulation, some relativistic effects have\n   662→recently been demonstrated in some controllable physical systems, such as graphene, semiconductor, superconductor and trapped ion [10–18]. It was recently considered that the ZB occurs not only under the relativistic\n   663→condition, but also extensively in the dynamics of a system with more than one degree of freedom [14, 19]. The\n   664→demonstration of the ZB beyond the relativistic electron\n   665→helps us to further understand the Dirac equation and\n   666→the relevant relativistic phenomena.\n   667→In this work, we focus on the role of parity played in the\n   668→simulation of Dirac equation for the ZB effect by a single\n   669→trapped ion. Like in [10, 16], we also employ the motional\n   670→degrees of freedom of the ion to simulate the position and\n   671→momentum of the relativistic electron, and the internal\n   672→degrees of freedom to refer to the energy states. Since\n   673→the motional state of the ion could be quantized, we may\n   674→discuss the problem in number-state representation. The\n   675→key point of our work is to introduce a parity operator Π̂,\n   676→\n   677→=== 1101.5182v2.pdf ===\n   678→Thermomechanics of DNA\n   679→Cristiano Nisoli and A. R. Bishop\n   680→Theoretical Division and Center for NonLinear Studies, Los Alamos National Laboratory, Los Alamos NM 87545 USA\n   681→(Dated: November 1, 2018)\n   682→A theory for thermomechanical behavior of homogeneous DNA at thermal equilibrium predicts\n   683→critical temperatures for denaturation under torque and stretch, phase diagrams for stable B–DNA,\n   684→supercoiling, optimally stable torque, and the overstretching transition as force-induced DNA melting. Agreement with available single molecule manipulation experiments is excellent.\n   685→\n   686→arXiv:1101.5182v2 [cond-mat.soft] 2 Feb 2011\n   687→\n   688→PACS numbers: 87.14.gk, 87.15.ad, 87.15.Zg,64.60.De\n   689→\n   690→DNA is a highly refined nanomechanical object. The\n   691→interplay between strong covalent bonds of the backbone\n   692→and weak hydrogen interactions between bases [1], the\n   693→thermal bath in which it is immersed, and the proximity\n   694→of physiological conditions to the denaturation temperature, make DNA highly and non-linearly responsive to\n   695→mechanical and thermal changes, and render any solely\n   696→mechanical approach inviable. This is critical for nanotechnology, where DNA is the basis for novel materials [2], but understanding double helix thermomechanics\n   697→would also illuminate biology, where enzymes involved in\n   698→replication and repair are viewed as molecular motors.\n   699→In the past twenty years, direct single molecule manipulation [3, 4] has revolutionized our understanding of key\n   700→aspects of DNA, revealing new couplings and transitions\n   701→between different structures, whose nature and forms,\n   702→however, are still speculative. When a few micrometer\n   703→long strand of DNA is stretched to a tension of the order\n   704→of pico-Newtons (pNs) to avoid formation of plectonemes,\n   705→sharp transitions are activated at positive and negative\n   706→torques, while an overstretching transition is observed\n   707→for DNA under tension of ≃ 60 pN at zero torque [5].\n   708→Tentative tension–torque phase diagrams for the stability of B–DNA, and various phenomenological theories,\n   709→have been proposed to explain these effects. The robustness of the Peyrard-Bishop-Dauxois approach (PBD)\n   710→[6–8] has been corroborated by Cocco and Barbi [9, 10]:\n   711→they incorporated torque and successfully reproduced denaturation by unwinding. However, these recent studies\n   712→do not include tension, do not explain denaturation at\n   713→overwinding, and do not provide phase diagrams in the\n   714→tension-torque plane. Also, although much simpler than\n   715→the molecular structure they describe, their complexity\n   716→still calls for numerical treatments, and cannot offer analytic equations to more easily guide experiments.\n   717→We address these issues by modeling the steric dependence of the base bond and the effect of tension and\n   718→torque in a way suitable for elimination of the angular degrees of freedom via integration of the partition function,\n   719→and obtain an effective energy for a PBD model which incorporates temperature and external loads. We compute\n   720→the phase diagram for B–DNA and the dependence of\n   721→supercoiling on torque, tension and temperature at criticality. Finally, we propose simple algebraic formulæ for\n   722→\n   723→the observables.\n   724→In our model i labels nucleotides separated by a distance a along the DNA backbone (Fig. 1), xi is the length\n   725→of the ith base’s bond, ωi = (θi+1 − θi−1 )/2 − Ω is the angular shift between nucleotides along the backbone, and\n   726→θi is their angular coordinate. As in the Cocco–Barbi\n   727→models the two strands of DNA are assumed symmetrical even when open. Ω denotes the natural pitch of the\n   728→\n   729→=== 1109.4653v1.pdf ===\n   730→Musimetrics\n   731→\n   732→Musimetrics\n   733→Vilson Vieira,1, a) Renato Fabbri,1, b) and Luciano da Fontoura Costa1, c)\n   734→Instituto de Fı́sica de São Carlos, Universidade de São Paulo (IFSC/USP)\n   735→\n   736→arXiv:1109.4653v1 [physics.data-an] 21 Sep 2011\n   737→\n   738→(Dated: 22 March 2019)\n   739→\n   740→Can the arts be analyzed in a quantitative manner? We propose a methodology to study music development by\n   741→applying multivariate statistics on composers characteristics. Seven representative composers were considered\n   742→in terms of eight main musical features. Grades were assigned to each characteristic and their correlations\n   743→were analyzed. A bootstrap method was applied to simulate hundreds of artificial composers influenced by the\n   744→seven representatives chosen. Applying dimensionality reduction we obtained a planar space used to quantify\n   745→non-numeric relations like dialectics, opposition and innovation. Composers differences on style and technique\n   746→were represented as geometrical distances in the planar space, making it possible to quantify, for example,\n   747→how much Bach and Stockhausen differ from other composers or how much Beethoven influenced Brahms.\n   748→In addition, we compared the results with a prior investigation on philosophy1 . The influence of dialectics,\n   749→strong on philosophy, was not remarkable on music. Instead, supporting an observation already considered\n   750→by music theorists, strong influences were identified between subsequent composers, implying inheritance and\n   751→suggesting a stronger master-disciple evolution when compared to the philosophy analysis.\n   752→PACS numbers: 89.75.Fb,05.65.+b\n   753→Keywords: music, musicology, pattern recognition, statistics\n   754→I.\n   755→\n   756→INTRODUCTION\n   757→\n   758→Along the history of music, composers developed their\n   759→own styles along a continuous search for coherence or\n   760→unity. In the words of Anton Webern2 , “[...] ever since\n   761→music has been written most great artists have striven to\n   762→make this unity ever clearer. Everything that has happened aims at this [...]”. Along this process we can identify a constantly heritage of style from one composer to\n   763→another as “a gradual development from its predecessor”,\n   764→contrasting with the necessity for innovation: “It is only\n   765→by experiment that progress is possible; it is the man\n   766→with the forward-looking type of mind [...] who forces\n   767→man out of the rut of ’what was good enough for my father is good enough for me’.”3 . Development in music\n   768→follows a dichotomy: while composers aims on innovation, creating their own styles, their technique is based\n   769→on the works of their predecessors, in a master-apprentice\n   770→tradition.\n   771→Other fields – like philosophy – demonstrate a welldefined trend when considering innovation: unlike music, the quest for difference seems to drive philosophical changes4 . Recently, this observation became more\n   772→evident with the application of a quantitative method1\n   773→where multivariate statistics was used to measure nonnumeric relations and to represent the historical development as time-series. More specifically, the method consists of scoring memorable philosophers based on some\n   774→relevant characteristics. The group of philosophers was\n   775→\n   776→a) http://automata.cc; Electronic mail: vilson@void.cc\n   777→b) http://www.estudiolivre.org/el-user.php?view user=gk;\n   778→\n   779→Elec-\n   780→\n   781→=== 1202.2518v4.pdf ===\n   782→Segmenting DNA sequence into ‘words’\n   783→Wang Liang, Zhao KaiYong\n   784→Tencent, SOSO, Beijing, P.R. China\n   785→Hong Kong Baptist University, Department of Computer Science, HK, P.R. China\n   786→*To whom correspondence should be addressed. E-mail:wangliang.f@gmail.com\n   787→\n   788→[Abstract] This paper presents a novel method to segment/decode DNA sequences based on\n   789→statistical language model. Firstly, we find the length of most DNA “words” is 12 to 15 bps by\n   790→analyzing the genomes of 12 model species. Then we apply the unsupervised approach to build the\n   791→DNA vocabulary and design DNA sequence segmentation method. We also find different genomes\n   792→is likely to use the similar ‘languages’.\n   793→\n   794→1 Introduction\n   795→The letters like A, T, C, G or protein A, R, N is still the basic units to analyze the DNA sequence.\n   796→Corresponding to English, there are 26 letters. If we only know English letters, we can’t give\n   797→deeply analyzing for English sequences. For example, “iloveapple” only contain few information.\n   798→We can’t connect it to significant terms. It’s also difficult to process letter sequence by computer.\n   799→We need word sequences “I/ love/ apple”. Most current information processing systems, such as\n   800→information retrieval, automatic proofreading, text classification, syntactic parser are all designed\n   801→in ‘words’ level, but not letter level.\n   802→The English sequence is naturally segmented by space. But for some languages like Chinese,\n   803→there is no space between letters. The fact that there are no delimiters in sequence posed well\n   804→know problem of word segmentation. The Chinese sequence is just like “iloveapple”, we need\n   805→segment it into “I/ love/ apple”. Segmentation is key step for most Chinese Information Processing\n   806→(CIP) systems.\n   807→DNA sequence is very similar to Chinese. These is also no any space or punctuation. So if we\n   808→could segment DNA sequence into DNA “words” sequence, we could apply many mature\n   809→information processing technologies to study DNA sequences. The DNA “words” sequence may\n   810→also give new hints to discovery the function of DNA.\n   811→This paper discuss this problem, how to divide DNA sequence into DNA ‘words’ sequence. We\n   812→refer to the Chinese segmentation research and design the DNA segmentation method. Normally,\n   813→there are two steps in these research. First, we should get a word list or vocabulary. Second, we\n   814→need design a sequence segmentation method based on vocabulary.\n   815→Because we do not have enough linguistic knowledge about DNA sequence, we apply\n   816→unsupervised methods to build the DNA vocabulary. These methods only need large raw corpus\n   817→(2,3,4). Fortunately, we have massive amounts of DNA information. This vocabulary building\n   818→methods will be discussed in paragraph 2. The following paragraph designs the DNA\n   819→\n   820→\fsegmentation\n   821→\n   822→method.\n   823→\n   824→For\n   825→\n   826→example,\n   827→\n   828→segmentation\n   829→\n   830→of\n   831→\n   832→\n   833→=== 1205.5651v1.pdf ===\n   834→Measuring the Evolution of Contemporary Western\n   835→Popular Music\n   836→\n   837→arXiv:1205.5651v1 [cs.SD] 25 May 2012\n   838→\n   839→Joan Serrà1 , Álvaro Corral2 , Marián Boguñá3 , Martı́n Haro4 , and Josep Ll. Arcos1\n   840→1 Artificial Intelligence Research Institute, Spanish National Research Council (IIIA-CSIC),\n   841→\n   842→Bellaterra, Barcelona, Spain.\n   843→2 Complex Systems Group, Centre de Recerca Matemàtica, Bellaterra, Barcelona, Spain.\n   844→3 Departament de Fı́sica Fonamental, Universitat de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain.\n   845→4 Music Technology Group, Universitat Pompeu Fabra, Barcelona, Spain.\n   846→\n   847→Abstract\n   848→Popular music is a key cultural expression that has captured listeners’ attention for\n   849→ages. Many of the structural regularities underlying musical discourse are yet to be discovered and, accordingly, their historical evolution remains formally unknown. Here we\n   850→unveil a number of patterns and metrics characterizing the generic usage of primary musical facets such as pitch, timbre, and loudness in contemporary western popular music.\n   851→Many of these patterns and metrics have been consistently stable for a period of more\n   852→than fifty years, thus pointing towards a great degree of conventionalism. Nonetheless,\n   853→we prove important changes or trends related to the restriction of pitch transitions, the\n   854→homogenization of the timbral palette, and the growing loudness levels. This suggests that\n   855→our perception of the new would be rooted on these changing characteristics. Hence, an old\n   856→tune could perfectly sound novel and fashionable, provided that it consisted of common\n   857→harmonic progressions, changed the instrumentation, and increased the average loudness.\n   858→\n   859→Introduction\n   860→Isn’t it always the same? This question could be easily posed while listening to the music of\n   861→any mainstream radio station in a western country. Like language, music is a human universal\n   862→involving perceptually discrete elements displaying organization [1]. Therefore, contemporary\n   863→popular music may have a well-established set of underlying patterns and regularities [1–4],\n   864→some of them potentially inherited from the classical tradition [5, 6]. Yet, as an incomparable artistic product for conveying emotions [7], music must incorporate variation over such\n   865→1\n   866→\n   867→\fpatterns in order to play upon people’s memories and expectations, making it attractive to\n   868→listeners [3, 4]. For the very same reasons, long-term variations of the underlying patterns\n   869→may also occur across years [8]. Many of these aspects remain formally unknown or lack scientific evidence, specially the latter, which is very often neglected in music-related studies,\n   870→from musicological analyses to technological applications. The study of patterns and long-term\n   871→variations in popular music could shed new light on relevant issues concerning its organization, structure, and dynamics [9]. More importantly, it addresses valuable questions for the\n   872→basic understanding of music as one of the main expressions of contemporary culture: Can\n   873→we identify some of the patterns behind music creation? Do musicians change them over the\n   874→years? What makes a popular tune novel to us? Is there an ‘evolution’ of musical discourse?\n   875→Current technologies for music information processing [10, 11] provide a unique opportunity to answer the above questions under objective, empirical, and quantitative premises.\n   876→Moreover, akin to recent advances in other cultural assets [12], they allow for unprecedented\n   877→large-scale analyses. One of the first publicly-available large-scale collections that has been\n   878→analyzed by standard music processing technologies is the million song dataset [13]. Among\n   879→others, the dataset includes the year annotations and audio descriptions of 464,411 distinct\n   880→music recordings (from 1955 to 2010), which roughly corresponds to more than 1,200 days of\n   881→continuous listening. Such recordings span a variety of popular genres, including rock, pop,\n   882→hip hop, metal, or electronic. Explicit descriptions available in the dataset [14] cover three\n   883→primary and complementary musical facets [2]: loudness, pitch, and timbre. Loudness basically correlates with our perception of sound amplitude or volume1 . Pitch roughly corresponds\n   884→\n   885→=== 1210.7091v2.pdf ===\n   886→Development of Hydrogen Bonding Magnetic Reaction-based Gene\n   887→Regulation through Cyclic Electromagnetic DNA Simulation in DoubleStranded DNA\n   888→\n   889→Suk Keun Lee1*, Dae Gwan Lee2, Yeon Sook Kim3\n   890→\n   891→1\n   892→\n   893→Department of Oral Pathology, College of Dentistry, Gangneung-Wonju National University, Gangneung;\n   894→and Institute of Hydrogen Bonding Magnetic Reaction Gene Regulation (Private research center, not\n   895→registrated), Daejeon, South Korea\n   896→\n   897→2\n   898→\n   899→Department of Mathematics and Big Data Science, Kumoh National Institute of Technology, Gumi, South\n   900→Korea\n   901→\n   902→3\n   903→\n   904→Department of Dental Hygiene, College of Health and Medical Sciences, Cheongju University, Cheongju,\n   905→South Korea\n   906→\n   907→Short title: Cyclic electromagnetic DNA simulation effect on dsDNA\n   908→\n   909→*Corresponding Authors:\n   910→Suk Keun Lee, DDS, MSD, PhD.\n   911→Department of Oral Pathology, College of Dentistry, Gangneung-Wonju National University, 7, Jukheon-gil,\n   912→Gangneung, 25457 South Korea.\n   913→E-mail : sukkeunlee2@naver.com\n   914→\n   915→1\n   916→\n   917→\fDevelopment of Hydrogen Bonding Magnetic Reaction-based Gene\n   918→Regulation through Cyclic Electromagnetic DNA Simulation in DoubleStranded DNA\n   919→Abstract\n   920→The proton-magnetic reaction is commonly used in MRI machines with a strong magnetic field of over 1 T,\n   921→while this study hypothesized that the electron magnetic reaction of hydrogen could affect the hydrogen bonds\n   922→of double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) at a low magnetic field below 0.01 T. The electron magnetic reaction in\n   923→hydrogen bonds is referred to as hydrogen bonding magnetic reaction (HBMR) in this study. The goal is to\n   924→develop a HBMR-based gene regulation (HBMR-GR) system, which can safely modulate the conformations and\n   925→functions of dsDNAs1. The polarities of DNA base pairs are derived from the relative electrostatic charge\n   926→between purines and pyrimidines, which become positively and negatively charged, respectively. The Pyu\n   927→dsDNAs with pyrimidine(s)-purine(s) sequences, ds3T3A, ds3C3G, and ds3C3A, showed stronger DNA\n   928→hybridization potential, increased infrared absorption at 3400-3200 cm-1, and a unique DNA conformation in\n   929→HPLC analysis compared to the corresponding Puy dsDNAs. To target the three-dimensional structure of\n   930→dsDNA based on the DNA base pair polarities, one can use cyclic electromagnetic DNA simulation (CEDS)\n   931→with approximately 25% efficiency for randomly oriented dsDNAs. This study found that the most effective\n   932→magnetic exposure times for inducing an electric potential in A-T and G-C base pairs were 280 and 480 msec,\n   933→respectively. CEDS was found to induce sequence-specific hybridization of target oligo-dsDNAs in 0.005M\n   934→NaCl solution and sequence-specific conformation of oligo-dsDNAs in 0.1M NaCl solution. It was found that\n   935→the Pyu oligo-dsDNAs were more responsible for the hybridization and conformational changes by CEDS than\n   936→\n   937→=== 1306.5298v2.pdf ===\n   938→Horizon wave-function for single localized particles:\n   939→GUP and quantum black hole decay\n   940→Roberto Casadio1, 2, ∗ and Fabio Scardigli3, 4, †\n   941→1\n   942→\n   943→arXiv:1306.5298v2 [gr-qc] 20 Oct 2013\n   944→\n   945→Dipartimento di Fisica e Astronomia, Università di Bologna, via Irnerio 46, 40126 Bologna, Italy\n   946→2\n   947→I.N.F.N., Sezione di Bologna, viale Berti Pichat 6/2, 40127 Bologna, Italy\n   948→3\n   949→Dipartimento di Matematica, Politecnico di Milano, Piazza L. da Vinci 32, 20133 Milano, Italy\n   950→4\n   951→Yukawa Institute for Theoretical Physics, Kyoto University, Kyoto 606-8502, Japan\n   952→\n   953→A localised particle in Quantum Mechanics is described by a wave packet in position space,\n   954→regardless of its energy. However, from the point of view of General Relativity, if the particle’s\n   955→energy density exceeds a certain threshold, it should be a black hole. In order to combine these two\n   956→pictures, we introduce a horizon wave-function determined by the particle wave-function in position\n   957→space, which eventually yields the probability that the particle is a black hole. The existence of a\n   958→minimum mass for black holes naturally follows, albeit not in the form of a sharp value around the\n   959→Planck scale, but rather like a vanishing probability that a particle much lighter than the Planck\n   960→mass be a black hole. We also show that our construction entails an effective Generalised Uncertainty\n   961→Principle (GUP), simply obtained by adding the uncertainties coming from the two wave-functions\n   962→associated to a particle. Finally, the decay of microscopic (quantum) black holes is also described\n   963→in agreement with what the GUP predicts.\n   964→PACS numbers: 04.70.Dy,04.70.-s,04.60.-m\n   965→\n   966→I.\n   967→\n   968→INTRODUCTION AND MOTIVATION\n   969→\n   970→Understanding all the physical aspects in the gravitational collapse of a compact object and how black holes\n   971→form, remains one of the most intriguing challenges of\n   972→contemporary theoretical physics. After the seminal papers of Oppenheimer and co-workers [1], the literature\n   973→on the subject has grown immensely, but many issues are\n   974→still open in General Relativity (see, e.g., Refs. [2, 3], and\n   975→references therein), not to mention the conceptual and\n   976→technical difficulties one faces when the quantum nature\n   977→of the collapsing matter is taken into account. Assuming quantum gravitational fluctuations are small, one can\n   978→describe matter by means of Quantum Field Theory on\n   979→the curved background space-time, an approach which\n   980→has produced remarkable results, but is unlikely to be directly applicable to a self-gravitating system representing\n   981→a collapsing object.\n   982→A general property of the Einstein theory is that the\n   983→gravitational interaction is always attractive and we are\n   984→thus not allowed to neglect its effect on the causal structure of space-time if we pack enough energy in a sufficiently small volume. This can occur, for example, if\n   985→two particles (for simplicity, of negligible spatial extension and total angular momentum) collide with an impact parameter b shorter than the Schwarzschild radius\n   986→corresponding to the total center-mass energy E of the\n   987→system, that is [31]\n   988→\n   989→=== 1308.3843v1.pdf ===\n   990→Coarse-graining DNA for simulations of DNA nanotechnology\n   991→Jonathan P. K. Doye,∗a Thomas E. Ouldridge,b Ard A. Louis,b Flavio Romano,a Petr Šulc,b Christian Matek,b Benedict E. K. Snodin,a Lorenzo Rovigatti,c John S. Schreck,a Ryan M. Harrisona and\n   992→William P. J. Smitha\n   993→Received Xth XXXXXXXXXX 20XX, Accepted Xth XXXXXXXXX 20XX\n   994→\n   995→arXiv:1308.3843v1 [q-bio.BM] 18 Aug 2013\n   996→\n   997→First published on the web Xth XXXXXXXXXX 200X\n   998→DOI: 10.1039/b000000x\n   999→\n  1000→To simulate long time and length scale processes involving DNA it is necessary to use a coarse-grained description. Here we\n  1001→provide an overview of different approaches to such coarse graining, focussing on those at the nucleotide level that allow the\n  1002→self-assembly processes associated with DNA nanotechnology to be studied. OxDNA, our recently-developed coarse-grained\n  1003→DNA model, is particularly suited to this task, and has opened up this field to systematic study by simulations. We illustrate\n  1004→some of the range of DNA nanotechnology systems to which the model is being applied, as well as the insights it can provide\n  1005→into fundamental biophysical properties of DNA.\n  1006→\n  1007→1\n  1008→\n  1009→Introduction\n  1010→\n  1011→DNA is one of the fundamental molecules of biology, enjoying iconic status in its role as the information storage medium\n  1012→for the genome. More recently, DNA has also become a\n  1013→startlingly successful nanoengineering material, with the exponents of DNA nanotechnology having developed an impressive array of DNA nanostructures 1 and nanodevices. 2 For\n  1014→both these uses of DNA, its physico-chemical properties are\n  1015→vital to its role.\n  1016→For example, the intra- and intermolecular interactions\n  1017→that drive base pairing and base stacking are responsible for\n  1018→DNA’s double helical structure. Furthermore, the selectivity\n  1019→of Watson-Crick base-paring and the efficiency with which\n  1020→double helices can self-assemble underlie both fundamental\n  1021→genomic processes, such as replication and transcription, and\n  1022→the programmability of DNA that has been exploited so successfully by DNA nanotechnology. On longer length scales,\n  1023→the mechanical properties of the double helix are important\n  1024→both to how DNA can be manipulated in the cellular environment, such as through the packaging of DNA by complexation with proteins and the control of its global topology by\n  1025→enzymes that modify its local twist, and in imparting rigidity\n  1026→to DNA nanostructures.\n  1027→Experimentally, there is an increasing wealth of information\n  1028→on DNA’s biophysical properties, particularly more recently\n  1029→from single-molecule experiments involving active manipulaa Physical & Theoretical Chemistry Laboratory, Department of Chemistry,\n  1030→\n  1031→University of Oxford, South Parks Road, Oxford, OX1 3QZ, UK.\n  1032→b Rudolf Peierls Centre for Theoretical Physics, 1 Keble Road, Oxford, OX1\n  1033→3NP, UK.\n  1034→c Dipartimento di Fisica, Università di Roma La Sapienza, Piazzale A. Moro\n  1035→2, 00185 Roma, Italy.\n  1036→\n  1037→tion by optical and magnetic tweezers 3,4 or by passive fluorescent probing of single molecules as they undergo dynamic\n  1038→processes. 5,6 However, the detailed microscopic mechanisms\n  1039→underlying the observed behaviour in these experiments are\n  1040→\n  1041→=== 1309.3658v2.pdf ===\n  1042→Title : Parallel DNA Synthesis : Two PCR product from one DNA template\n  1043→Bhardwaj Vikash1 and Sharma Kulbhushan2\n  1044→1\n  1045→\n  1046→Email: vikashbhardwaj@ gmail.com\n  1047→\n  1048→1\n  1049→\n  1050→Current address: Government College Sector 14 Gurgaon, Haryana, India\n  1051→\n  1052→2\n  1053→\n  1054→Email: kulsviro@gmail.com\n  1055→\n  1056→2\n  1057→\n  1058→Current address: MCS Group, Institute of Nuclear Medicine and Allied Sciences,\n  1059→\n  1060→Timarpur, Delhi, India\n  1061→\n  1062→Abstract: Conventionally in a PCR reaction, Primers binds to DNA template in an\n  1063→antiparallel manner and template DNA is amplified as it is. Here we describe an\n  1064→approach in which First primer binds in a complementary parallel orientation leading\n  1065→to synthesis in parallel direction. Further reaction happened in usual way leading to\n  1066→synthesis of final DNA product having opposite polarity then the template used. We\n  1067→first time proved that synthesis of DNA can happen in a parallel orientation. We first\n  1068→time have shown that from a single DNA template, Two different related PCR\n  1069→products can be synthesized.\n  1070→\n  1071→Our fundamental knowledge of DNA structure is based on the Watson – Crick model\n  1072→of DNA double helix which is able to explain key processes that are essential for life.\n  1073→Conformational polymorphism of DNA is now extending beyond the Watson - Crick\n  1074→double helix. In addition to the families of the A, B and Z forms (Double helices with\n  1075→antiparallel orientation of strands ) existence of DNA double helix with parallel strand\n  1076→(ps-DNA) was proven (1,2,3). For instanance, Tchurikov et al in 1986 have founded\n  1077→that fragments of short dispersed and actively transcribed suffix non coding exon\n  1078→\n  1079→\fsequence element (from 48 to 88 bp) of Drosophilla are complementary in a parallel\n  1080→orientation to the fragment of the 5’ non coding sequence in adult mRNA of the\n  1081→alcohol dehydrogenase gene (from +47 to + 88 bp). There are three insertion in each\n  1082→sequence as well as four non complementary pairs. Thus, the parallel DNA strands\n  1083→of the suffix (41 bp) and alcohol dehydrogenase (42 bp) was found to have 32\n  1084→complementary nucleotides (4). Such mutual orientation of strands and non watson\n  1085→crick base pairing can arise at low pH, as a consequnce of Chemical modifications of\n  1086→nucleotide bases or sugar – phosphate back-bone or upon ligand binding (2). Mirror\n  1087→repeats able to form parallel stranded DNA with AT, GC pairs are abundant in\n  1088→genomes of various organisms (5). The base pairs schemes have been established\n  1089→with Raman and FTIR spectroscopy and then verified by NMR. In parallel stranded\n  1090→DNA, AT pairs with two hydrogen bonds were found to be of reversed Watson –Crick\n  1091→type. Bases of a GC pairs in parallel stranded DNA are somewhat shifted relative to\n  1092→\n  1093→=== 1311.5931v2.pdf ===\n  1094→Black hole remnant of black hole-neutron star coalescing binaries with arbitrary black hole spin\n  1095→Francesco Pannarale∗\n  1096→School of Physics and Astronomy, Cardiff University, The Parade, Cardiff CF24 3AA, United Kingdom\n  1097→Max-Planck-Institut für Gravitationsphysik, Albert Einstein Institut, Potsdam 14476, Germany\n  1098→A model for determining the dimensionless spin parameter and mass of the black hole remnant of black holeneutron star mergers with arbitrary initial black hole spin angular momentum, binary mass ratio, and neutron\n  1099→star mass and cold equation of state is formulated. Tests against numerical-relativity results are carried out,\n  1100→showing that both the dimensionless spin parameter and the final mass are accurately reproduced. For the first\n  1101→time, the behavior of both quantities and of the l = 2, m = 2, n = 0 quasinormal mode frequency is inspected\n  1102→throughout the parameter space. Predictions of this frequency may be exploited to guide gravitational-wave\n  1103→modeling and detection efforts and to extract physical information from detected gravitational-wave signals that\n  1104→would help us break degeneracies between binary black hole and black hole-neutron star systems, improve our\n  1105→understanding of compact binary formation, and constrain the neutron star equation of state.\n  1106→PACS numbers: 04.25.dk, 04.30.Db, 95.30.Sf, 97.60.Jd\n  1107→\n  1108→arXiv:1311.5931v2 [gr-qc] 10 Mar 2014\n  1109→\n  1110→I.\n  1111→\n  1112→INTRODUCTION\n  1113→\n  1114→Following its formation, a black hole-neutron star (BH-NS)\n  1115→binary system loses orbital energy and angular momentum by\n  1116→emitting gravitational radiation. During the inspiral stage, it\n  1117→readjusts its orbital separation accordingly, until the energy\n  1118→reservoir is emptied and the two compact objects merge. The\n  1119→remnant of this coalescence consists of a black hole possibly\n  1120→surrounded by a hot, massive accretion torus [1]. BH-NS binaries have not been observed yet, but population synthesis\n  1121→studies suggest that BH-NS mergers are likely to occur frequently in the Hubble volume [2–5]. By detecting the radiation emitted during their inspiral and merger, gravitationalwave (GW) observatories — such as LIGO [6], Virgo [7],\n  1122→KAGRA [8], and the Einstein Telescope [9] — are therefore likely to be involved in the first direct observations of\n  1123→BH-NS systems. Advanced LIGO is expected to see between\n  1124→0.2 and 300 BH-NS binaries per year [10]. Other than being\n  1125→GW sources [11–14], BH-NS binaries, or mixed binaries, are\n  1126→among promising progenitor candidates for a fraction of the\n  1127→short-hard gamma-ray bursts (SGRBs) we observe [15, 16].\n  1128→Further, NSs in BH-NS systems may undergo tidal distortions\n  1129→and disruptions. This aspect has drawn considerable attention in relation to the possibility of constraining the equation of state (EOS) of matter at supranuclear densities [17–\n  1130→20] and in relation to the physical processes the merger ejecta\n  1131→may be involved in. Radioactive decays triggered in the outflows by the formation of heavy isotopes may power day-long\n  1132→optical transients (“kilonovæ”), and shocks generated by the\n  1133→outflows themselves in an interstellar medium of sufficiently\n  1134→high density may also lead to visible electromagnetic counterparts [21, 22]. Additionally, the ejecta are believed to\n  1135→be linked to the abundances of heavy elements formed in rprocesses, by rapid neutron capture [23].\n  1136→Numerical-relativity dynamical simulations of BH-NS\n  1137→mergers will ultimately allow us to fully understand and explore the physics behind these events. Research with this\n  1138→\n  1139→∗ francesco.pannarale@ligo.org\n  1140→\n  1141→intent was initiated fairly recently [24–54] and has benefitted from relativistic quasiequilibrium studies [55–61]. As\n  1142→fully relativistic dynamical simulations remain challenging\n  1143→and computationally intensive, pseudo-Newtonian calculations, e.g. [62], and analytical studies, e.g. [17–19, 63–71],\n  1144→\n  1145→=== 1312.2849v3.pdf ===\n  1146→Lamata et al.\n  1147→\n  1148→RESEARCH\n  1149→\n  1150→Efficient quantum simulation of fermionic and\n  1151→bosonic models in trapped ions\n  1152→arXiv:1312.2849v3 [quant-ph] 18 Jun 2014\n  1153→\n  1154→L. Lamata1 , A. Mezzacapo1 , J. Casanova1 and E. Solano1,2\n  1155→Full list of author information is\n  1156→available at the end of the article\n  1157→\n  1158→Abstract\n  1159→We analyze the efficiency of quantum simulations of fermionic and bosonic\n  1160→models in trapped ions. In particular, we study the optimal time of entangling\n  1161→gates and the required number of total elementary gates. Furthermore, we\n  1162→exemplify these estimations in the light of quantum simulations of quantum field\n  1163→theories, condensed-matter physics, and quantum chemistry. Finally, we show\n  1164→that trapped-ion technologies are a suitable platform for implementing quantum\n  1165→simulations involving interacting fermionic and bosonic modes, paving the way for\n  1166→overcoming classical computers in the near future.\n  1167→03.67.Ac, 03.67.Lx, 37.10.Ty\n  1168→Keywords: Quantum Information; Quantum Simulation; Trapped Ions\n  1169→\n  1170→1 Background\n  1171→Quantum simulation is one of the most promising fields in quantum information\n  1172→science. Feynman already pointed out in 1982 [1] that a controllable quantum platform could simulate the dynamics or static properties of another quantum system\n  1173→exponentially faster than classical computers. Since then, this hypothesis has been\n  1174→demonstrated [2], and important theoretical and experimental work followed [3–5].\n  1175→Furthermore, quantum simulators establish analogies between previously unconnected fields, and have as a main aim to overcome classical computers.\n  1176→Many proposals and experimental realizations of quantum simulations in a broad\n  1177→variety of platforms have been put forward, as for example spin systems [6–9], the\n  1178→Bose-Hubbard model implemented with cold atoms [10], quantum chemistry [11]\n  1179→and quantum statistics [12–14] simulated with photonic systems, condensed matter\n  1180→models with Rydberg atoms [15], relativistic quantum mechanics [16–19], quantum field theories [20], and the lattice Schwinger model [21]. On the other hand,\n  1181→quantum simulations of fermionic and bosonic systems in trapped ions have been\n  1182→recently proposed [22]. Therefore, it is timely to study the experimental requirements needed, to assess the feasibility of the proposal and to compare it with other\n  1183→implementations.\n  1184→In this article, we analyze the necessary resources to implement a quantum simulation of fermions and bosons with trapped ions [20, 22–24]. We show that the\n  1185→methods developed for simulating fermionic and bosonic systems with ions can save\n  1186→a large amount of resources in terms of gates with respect to other platforms. This\n  1187→demonstrates that trapped ions are a promising quantum technology for a wide\n  1188→variety of quantum simulations, including high energy physics, condensed matter,\n  1189→or quantum chemistry.\n  1190→\n  1191→\fLamata et al.\n  1192→\n  1193→Page 2 of 13\n  1194→\n  1195→Trapped-ion systems are one of the most advanced technologies for implementing\n  1196→\n  1197→=== 1401.4725v1.pdf ===\n  1198→Information profiles for DNA pattern discovery\n  1199→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"9e94d7ca-42d4-4d4c-a2d0-4d94c96d7671","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:03:32.128Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1ce7f32e-a51c-4439-88d4-89a5fa4566a8"}
