{"parentUuid":"1944d619-d6dc-43ac-8ab9-9d9cf469ce2e","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a5de01b","slug":"lovely-wishing-lantern","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_vrtx_01FwpgftfDPHYeYgHDCPopkt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_017wCHQSX67MF9kpEtRbtnYE","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-root/2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_017qp5RfQsP2SwYRFpmmRwc6.txt","offset":1200,"limit":600}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":77822,"cache_read_input_tokens":10918,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":77822,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXb5rJCBBBi55HveqRb1J","type":"assistant","uuid":"c98a790f-f05f-47f6-a29d-b542763fbf7e","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:03:37.842Z"}
{"parentUuid":"c98a790f-f05f-47f6-a29d-b542763fbf7e","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a5de01b","slug":"lovely-wishing-lantern","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_017wCHQSX67MF9kpEtRbtnYE","type":"tool_result","content":"  1200→arXiv:1401.4725v1 [q-bio.GN] 19 Jan 2014\n  1201→\n  1202→Armando J. Pinho, Diogo Pratas, and Paulo J. S. G. Ferreira\n  1203→IEETA / Dept of Electronics, Telecommunications and Informatics\n  1204→University of Aveiro, 3810–193 Aveiro, Portugal\n  1205→ap@ua.pt — pratas@ua.pt — pjf@ua.pt\n  1206→\n  1207→Abstract\n  1208→Finite-context modeling is a powerful tool for compressing and hence for representing DNA\n  1209→sequences. We describe an algorithm to detect genomic regularities, within a blind discovery\n  1210→strategy. The algorithm uses information profiles built using suitable combinations of finitecontext models. We used the genome of the fission yeast Schizosaccharomyces pombe strain\n  1211→972 h− for illustration, unveiling locations of low information content, which are usually\n  1212→associated with DNA regions of potential biological interest.\n  1213→\n  1214→Introduction\n  1215→Graphical representations of DNA sequences are a handy way of quickly finding regions of potential interest. This has been a topic addressed using various approaches\n  1216→(see, for example, [1–10]), some of them relying on information theoretical principles.\n  1217→Both global and local estimates of the randomness of a sequence provide useful information, but both also have shortcomings. Global estimates do not show how the\n  1218→characteristics change along the sequence. Local estimates fail to take into consideration the global properties of the sequence. This latter drawback was addressed by\n  1219→Clift et al. [11] using the concept of sequence landscape, plots displaying the number\n  1220→of times oligonucleotides from the target sequence occur in a given source sequence.\n  1221→If the target and source sequences coincide, then the landscape provides information\n  1222→about self-similarities (repeats) of the target sequence.\n  1223→The sequence landscapes of Clift et al. [11] have been a first attempt to display\n  1224→local information, taking into account global characteristics of the sequence. This\n  1225→idea was pursed by Allison et al. [12] using XM, a model that considers a sequence as\n  1226→a mixture of regions with little structure and regions that are approximate repeats.\n  1227→With this statistical model, they have produced information sequences, which quantify the amount of surprise of having a given base at a given position, knowing the\n  1228→remaining of the sequence. When plotted, one of these information sequences provides\n  1229→a quick overview of certain properties of the original symbolic sequence, allowing for\n  1230→example to easily identify zones of rich repetitive content [13–15].\n  1231→The information sequences of Allison et al. [12] are deeply related to data compression. The role of data compression for pattern discovery in DNA sequences was\n  1232→initially pointed out by Grumbach et al. [16] and, since then, it has been pursued\n  1233→by other researchers (e.g. [13, 17]). In fact, the algorithmic information content of a\n  1234→sequence is the size, in bits, of the shortest description of the sequence.\n  1235→In this paper, we propose using combinations of several finite-context models, each\n  1236→of a different depth, for building information profiles. Such models have been shown\n  1237→\n  1238→\fto adequately capture the statistical properties of DNA sequences [18–21] but are\n  1239→direction-dependent, i.e., the results depend on which direction the DNA sequence\n  1240→is processed. We remove this directional dependency by combining the amount of\n  1241→information that a certain DNA base carries in each processing direction.\n  1242→The information profiles are found using an algorithm based on finite-context\n  1243→models that needs time proportional to the length of the sequence. We present a\n  1244→proof-of-concept study of the potential of information profiles in genome analysis,\n  1245→namely, for detecting genomic structural and functional regularities. We uncover\n  1246→genomic regularities on a large-scale, such as, centromeric and telomeric regions of\n  1247→a chromosome, or transposable elements. In this context, we use the genome of the\n  1248→\n  1249→=== 1402.5127v3.pdf ===\n  1250→Black holes and thermodynamics — The first\n  1251→half century\n  1252→\n  1253→arXiv:1402.5127v3 [gr-qc] 17 Mar 2014\n  1254→\n  1255→Daniel Grumiller, Robert McNees and Jakob Salzer\n  1256→\n  1257→Abstract Black hole thermodynamics emerged from the classical general relativistic laws of black hole mechanics, summarized by Bardeen–Carter–Hawking, together with the physical insights by Bekenstein about black hole entropy and the\n  1258→semi-classical derivation by Hawking of black hole evaporation. The black hole\n  1259→entropy law inspired the formulation of the holographic principle by ’t Hooft and\n  1260→Susskind, which is famously realized in the gauge/gravity correspondence by Maldacena, Gubser–Klebanov–Polaykov and Witten within string theory. Moreover, the\n  1261→microscopic derivation of black hole entropy, pioneered by Strominger–Vafa within\n  1262→string theory, often serves as a consistency check for putative theories of quantum\n  1263→gravity. In this book chapter we review these developments over five decades, starting in the 1960ies.\n  1264→\n  1265→Introduction and Prehistory\n  1266→Introductory remarks. The history of black hole thermodynamics is intertwined\n  1267→with the history of quantum gravity. In the absence of experimental data capable of\n  1268→probing Planck scale physics the best we can do is to subject putative theories of\n  1269→quantum gravity to stringent consistency checks. Black hole thermodynamics provides a number of highly non-trivial consistency checks. Perhaps most famously,\n  1270→Daniel Grumiller\n  1271→Institute for Theoretical Physics, Vienna University of Technology, Wiedner Hauptstrasse 8-10,\n  1272→A-1040 Vienna, Austria e-mail: grumil@hep.itp.tuwien.ac.at\n  1273→Robert McNees\n  1274→Loyola University Chicago, Department of Physics, Chicago, IL 60660, USA e-mail:\n  1275→rmcnees@luc.edu\n  1276→Jakob Salzer\n  1277→Institute for Theoretical Physics, Vienna University of Technology, Wiedner Hauptstrasse 8-10,\n  1278→A-1040 Vienna, Austria e-mail: salzer@hep.itp.tuwien.ac.at\n  1279→\n  1280→1\n  1281→\n  1282→\f2\n  1283→\n  1284→Daniel Grumiller, Robert McNees and Jakob Salzer\n  1285→\n  1286→any theory of quantum gravity that fails to reproduce the Bekenstein–Hawking relation\n  1287→kB c3 A h\n  1288→SBH =\n  1289→(1)\n  1290→4h̄G\n  1291→between the black hole entropy SBH , the area of the event horizon Ah , and Newton’s\n  1292→constant G would be regarded with a great amount of skepticism (see e.g [1]).\n  1293→In addition to providing a template for the falsification of speculative models\n  1294→of quantum gravity, black hole thermodynamics has also sparked essential developments in the field of quantum gravity and remains a vital source of insight and\n  1295→new ideas. Discussions about information loss, the holographic principle, the microscopic origin of black hole entropy, gravity as an emergent phenomenon, and\n  1296→the more recent firewall paradox all have roots in black hole thermodynamics. Furthermore, it is an interesting subject in its own right, with unusual behavior of specific heat, a rich phenomenology, and remarkable phase transitions between different spacetimes.\n  1297→In this review we summarize the development of black hole thermodynamics\n  1298→chronologically, except when the narrative demands deviations from a strictly historical account. While we have tried to be comprehensive, our coverage is limited\n  1299→by a number of factors, not the least of which is our own knowledge of the literature\n  1300→\n  1301→=== 1403.1523v2.pdf ===\n  1302→A Novel Method for Comparative Analysis of DNA\n  1303→Sequences by Ramanujan-Fourier Transform\n  1304→\n  1305→arXiv:1403.1523v2 [cs.CE] 27 Jun 2014\n  1306→\n  1307→Changchuan Yin1,∗ , Xuemeng E. Yin2 , Jiasong Wang3\n  1308→1.College of Information Systems and Technology, University of Phoenix, Chicago, IL\n  1309→60601, USA\n  1310→2.William Fremd High School, Palatine, IL 60067, USA\n  1311→3.Department of Mathematics, Nanjing University, Nanjing, Jiangsu 210093, China\n  1312→∗ Corresponding author, Email: cyinbox@email.phoenix.edu\n  1313→\n  1314→Abstract\n  1315→Alignment-free sequence analysis approaches provide important alternatives\n  1316→over multiple sequence alignment (MSA) in biological sequence analysis because alignment-free approaches have low computation complexity and are\n  1317→not dependent on high level of sequence identity, however, most of the existing alignment-free methods do not employ true full information content\n  1318→of sequences and thus can not accurately reveal similarities and diﬀerences\n  1319→among DNA sequences. We present a novel alignment-free computational\n  1320→method for sequence analysis based on Ramanujan-Fourier transform (RFT),\n  1321→in which complete information of DNA sequences is retained. We represent\n  1322→DNA sequences as four binary indicator sequences and apply RFT on the\n  1323→indicator sequences to convert them into frequency domain. The Euclidean\n  1324→distance of the complete RFT coeﬃcients of DNA sequences are used as\n  1325→similarity measure. To address the diﬀerent lengths in Euclidean space of\n  1326→RFT coeﬃcients, we pad zeros to short DNA binary sequences so that the\n  1327→binary sequences equal the longest length in the comparison sequence data.\n  1328→Thus, the DNA sequences are compared in the same dimensional frequency\n  1329→space without information loss. We demonstrate the usefulness of the proposed method by presenting experimental results on hierarchical clustering of\n  1330→genes and genomes. The proposed method opens a new channel to biological\n  1331→sequence analysis, classiﬁcation, and structural module identiﬁcation.\n  1332→Keywords: Ramanujan-Fourier transform, Similarity measure,\n  1333→Phylogenetic trees, Cluster analysis, DNA sequence\n  1334→\n  1335→Preprint submitted to arXiv.org\n  1336→\n  1337→June 30, 2014\n  1338→\n  1339→\f1. Introduction\n  1340→Comparative analysis of DNA sequences is a fundamental task in genome\n  1341→research. Multiple sequence alignment (MSA) has assumed a key role in comparative structure and function analysis of biological sequences, but MSA\n  1342→has high computation complexity and is dependent on high level of sequence\n  1343→identify. Several alignment-free sequence comparison methods have been\n  1344→proposed to address the problems limitations of MSA. These alignment-free\n  1345→methods can be divided into categories that include feature frequency proﬁles\n  1346→(FFP) (Sims et al., 2009), information theories (Li et al., 2001), graphic theory (Qi et al., 2011), and analysis in frequency domain (Zhao et al., 2011).\n  1347→In these methods, feature extraction or transformation of a sequence may\n  1348→incur loss of information and may not reﬂect the actual similarities and differences among DNA sequences. It has been one of major challenges in DNA\n  1349→sequence analysis to create accurate and eﬃcient alignment-free methods for\n  1350→proper measurements of sequence similarity (Vinga and Almeida, 2003). To\n  1351→this end, we explore a digital signal processing approach that may retain full\n  1352→\n  1353→=== 1403.4513v1.pdf ===\n  1354→arXiv:1403.4513v1 [stat.AP] 14 Nov 2013\n  1355→\n  1356→A quantitative approach to evolution of music and\n  1357→philosophy\n  1358→Vilson Vieira1 , Renato Fabbri1 , Gonzalo Travieso1 , Osvaldo N.\n  1359→Oliveira Jr.1 , Luciano da Fontoura Costa1\n  1360→1\n  1361→\n  1362→Instituto de Fı́sica de São Carlos, Universidade de São Paulo (IFSC/USP)\n  1363→\n  1364→E-mail: vilsonvieira@usp.br, fabbri@usp.br, gonzalo@ifsc.usp.br,\n  1365→chu@ifsc.usp.br, ldfcosta@gmail.com\n  1366→Abstract. The development of new statistical and computational methods is\n  1367→increasingly making it possible to bridge the gap between hard sciences and humanities.\n  1368→In this study, we propose an approach based on a quantitative evaluation of attributes\n  1369→of objects in fields of humanities, from which concepts such as dialectics and\n  1370→opposition are formally defined mathematically. As case studies, we analyzed the\n  1371→temporal evolution of classical music and philosophy by obtaining data for 8 features\n  1372→characterizing the corresponding fields for 7 well-known composers and philosophers,\n  1373→which were treated with multivariate statistics and pattern recognition methods. A\n  1374→bootstrap method was applied to avoid statistical bias caused by the small sample\n  1375→data set, with which hundreds of artificial composers and philosophers were generated,\n  1376→influenced by the 7 names originally chosen. Upon defining indices for opposition,\n  1377→skewness and counter-dialectics, we confirmed the intuitive analysis of historians in that\n  1378→classical music evolved according to a master-apprentice tradition, while in philosophy\n  1379→changes were driven by opposition. Though these case studies were meant only to\n  1380→show the possibility of treating phenomena in humanities quantitatively, including\n  1381→a quantitative measure of concepts such as dialectics and opposition the results are\n  1382→encouraging for further application of the approach presented here to many other\n  1383→areas, since it is entirely generic.\n  1384→\n  1385→PACS numbers: 89.75.Fb,05.65.+b\n  1386→\n  1387→Keywords: Arts, music, musicology, philosophy, pattern recognition, statistics\n  1388→\n  1389→\fA quantitative approach to evolution of music and philosophy\n  1390→\n  1391→2\n  1392→\n  1393→1. Introduction\n  1394→Philosophy and natural sciences were born together in the 6th century B.C. within\n  1395→Greek civilization [1]. Logic started with Aristotle, geometry with Thales and Euclid,\n  1396→arithmetic with Diophantus, while others created algebra, astronomy and politics, with\n  1397→all fields being part of a common knowledge space [2]. What we know today as modern\n  1398→philosophy and science were not originally separate, and arts were also present almost\n  1399→universally in this space, not only as cultural expression. The harmonic properties of\n  1400→music were of interest to philosophers such as Pythagoras. Aristotle with his Poetics\n  1401→discussed a dramatic theory for theatre. In this multidisciplinary space, music was\n  1402→addressed with mathematics and science with philosophy. Quantitative methods were\n  1403→applied to explain humanities, while the scientific method had its origin in philosophy.\n  1404→\n  1405→=== 1404.2126v1.pdf ===\n  1406→Black Hole Chemistry\n  1407→D. Kubizňák1 R. B. Mann2\n  1408→Perimeter Institute for Theoretical Physics\n  1409→\n  1410→arXiv:1404.2126v1 [gr-qc] 8 Apr 2014\n  1411→\n  1412→35 Caroline St. Waterloo, Ontario, Canada, N2L 2Y5\n  1413→& Department of Physics and Astronomy, University of Waterloo\n  1414→200 University Ave., Waterloo, Ontario N2L 3G1\n  1415→Essay written for the Gravity Research Foundation 2014 Awards for Essays on Gravitation\n  1416→Submitted March 31, 2014\n  1417→Abstract\n  1418→The mass of a black hole has traditionally been identified with its energy. We describe a\n  1419→new perspective on black hole thermodynamics, one that identifies the mass of a black hole\n  1420→with chemical enthalpy, and the cosmological constant as thermodynamic pressure. This leads\n  1421→to an understanding of black holes from the viewpoint of chemistry, in terms of concepts such\n  1422→as Van der Waals fluids, reentrant phase transitions, and triple points. Both charged and rotating\n  1423→black holes exhibit novel chemical-type phase behaviour, hitherto unseen.\n  1424→\n  1425→1\n  1426→2\n  1427→\n  1428→Email: dkubiznak@perimeterinstitute.ca\n  1429→Email: rbmann@uwaterloo.ca\n  1430→\n  1431→1\n  1432→\n  1433→\fOur best window as to the underlying structure of quantum gravity comes from black hole\n  1434→thermodynamics. By now the parallel with standard thermodynamics is well-known:\n  1435→\n  1436→Energy\n  1437→\n  1438→E ↔ M\n  1439→\n  1440→Temperature\n  1441→\n  1442→T\n  1443→\n  1444→↔\n  1445→\n  1446→Entropy\n  1447→\n  1448→S\n  1449→\n  1450→↔\n  1451→\n  1452→Mass\n  1453→\n  1454→κ\n  1455→Surface Gravity\n  1456→\n  1457→=== 1411.1974v2.pdf ===\n  1458→A generalized Jaynes-Cummings model: The relativistic\n  1459→parametric amplifier and a single trapped ion\n  1460→arXiv:1411.1974v2 [hep-th] 24 Jun 2016\n  1461→\n  1462→D. Ojeda-Guilléna,∗\n  1463→R. D. Motab and V. D. Granadosc\n  1464→a Escuela Superior de Cómputo, Instituto Politécnico Nacional, Av. Juan de Dios Bátiz\n  1465→\n  1466→esq. Av. Miguel Othón de Mendizábal, Col. Lindavista, Delegación Gustavo A. Madero,\n  1467→C.P. 07738, Ciudad de México, Mexico.\n  1468→b\n  1469→\n  1470→Escuela Superior de Ingenierı́a Mecánica y Eléctrica, Unidad Culhuacán, Instituto\n  1471→Politécnico Nacional, Av. Santa Ana No. 1000, Col. San Francisco Culhuacán,\n  1472→Delegación Coyoacán, C.P. 04430, Ciudad de México, Mexico.\n  1473→c\n  1474→\n  1475→Escuela Superior de Fı́sica y Matemáticas, Instituto Politécnico Nacional, Ed. 9,\n  1476→Unidad Profesional Adolfo López Mateos, Delegación Gustavo A. Madero, C.P. 07738,\n  1477→Ciudad de México, Mexico.\n  1478→\n  1479→(Date of resubmission: 1 June 2016.)\n  1480→Abstract\n  1481→We introduce a generalization of the Jaynes-Cummings model and study some of its\n  1482→properties. We obtain the energy spectrum and eigenfunctions of this model by using\n  1483→the tilting transformation and the squeezed number states of the one-dimensional harmonic oscillator. As physical applications, we connect this new model to two important\n  1484→and novelty problems: the relativistic parametric amplifier and the quantum simulation\n  1485→of a single trapped ion.\n  1486→\n  1487→PACS: 03.65.Fd, 02.20.Sv, 42.50.Ar, 42.65.Yj\n  1488→Keywords: Jaynes-Cummings, squeezed states, tilting transformation, degenerate parametric\n  1489→amplifier.\n  1490→\n  1491→1\n  1492→\n  1493→Introduction\n  1494→\n  1495→The Dirac oscillator was first introduced by Ito et al. [1] and Cook [2] and reintroduced in\n  1496→the 80’s by Moshinsky and Szczepaniak [3]. They added the linear term −imcωβα · r to the\n  1497→relativistic momentum p of the free-particle Dirac equation. This problem reduces to the\n  1498→harmonic oscillator plus a spin-orbit coupling term in the non-relativistic limit.\n  1499→∗\n  1500→\n  1501→E-mail address: dojedag@ipn.mx\n  1502→\n  1503→1\n  1504→\n  1505→\fThe Dirac-Moshinsky oscillator has been applied to quark confinement models in quantum chromodynamics, hexagonal lattices and the emulation of graphene in electromagnetic\n  1506→billiards [4], among others. In particular, in the 1+1-dimensional space, the Dirac-Moshinsky\n  1507→oscillator has been exactly solved by using the theory of the non-relativistic harmonic oscillator [5, 6]. Moreover, Nogami and Toyama constructed the relativistic coherent state for this\n  1508→\n  1509→=== 1501.07133v2.pdf ===\n  1510→1\n  1511→\n  1512→On Optimal Family of Codes for Archival DNA\n  1513→Storage\n  1514→Dixita Limbachiya, Vijay Dhameliya, Madhav Khakhar and Manish K. Gupta\n  1515→Dhirubhai Ambani Institute of Information and Communication Technology\n  1516→Gandhinagar, Gujarat, 382007 India\n  1517→Email: dlimbachiya@acm.org and mankg@computer.org\n  1518→\n  1519→Abstract\n  1520→\n  1521→arXiv:1501.07133v2 [cs.IT] 22 Apr 2016\n  1522→\n  1523→1\n  1524→\n  1525→DNA based storage systems received attention by many researchers. This includes archival and re-writable random access\n  1526→DNA based storage systems. In this work, we have developed an efficient technique to encode the data into DNA sequence by\n  1527→using non-linear families of ternary codes. In particular, we proposes an algorithm to encode data into DNA with high information\n  1528→storage density and better error correction using a sub code of Golay code. Theoretically, 115 exabytes (EB) data can be stored\n  1529→in one gram of DNA by our method.\n  1530→\n  1531→I. I NTRODUCTION\n  1532→Big data explosion due to advancement in social networking and IoT (Internet of Things) produces immense data, which\n  1533→urge the data scientists to strive for the development of better data storage medium. Optical, digital and cloud data storage\n  1534→[2] medium have their own limitations and need to be maintained regularly. While the computer scientists are endeavoring\n  1535→to develop dense data storage medium, researchers at the other end thought of exploring natural medium to safeguard this\n  1536→data.Properties of DNA like scalability, density and long term stability makes it ideal for long term archival of data. A good\n  1537→deal of work has been done to store the data on DNA [3] [4] [5] [6]. By employing error correction for DNA data storage, the\n  1538→breakthrough was made by N. Goldman and his team [7] in the year 2013. They developed very efficient and novel approach\n  1539→for storing the data of size 739 KB on DNA and retrieving it back. They used four folds redundancy to tackle errors by\n  1540→retrieving correct data from one of the copies of DNA strands. But due to redundancy there was increase in the length of DNA\n  1541→which makes the technique expensive to use DNA for storage at the commercial level. In the year 2014, group at The Chinese\n  1542→University of Hong Kong developed method for larger DNA reads used to embed the data [8]. Low Density Parity-Check\n  1543→(LDPC) codes based method was introduced by Aldrin Kay-Yuen Yim et al., to encode data in DNA for improved length of\n  1544→DNA reads. Most of the methods used so far have limitations in size of the data inserted in DNA and cost associated with\n  1545→DNA synthesis and sequencing technology. Work of Goldman [7] and Church [6] has laid a cornerstone for DNA based data\n  1546→storage systems. Recently model of DNA channel for data storage is proposed by Han Mao Kiah et al., [9]. Also a rewritable\n  1547→random access DNA based storage system has been very recently reported [1]. In [10], Reed Solomon codes based DNA\n  1548→storage system has been reported. In this work, we have developed error correction scheme (a family of non linear ternary\n  1549→codes) by modifying Goldman’s scheme [7] for the error detection and correction along with improvement in storage capacity\n  1550→to store data on DNA.\n  1551→The paper is organized as follows. Section 2 describes briefly related work and the Golddman’s approach. Section 3 gives\n  1552→our work on non-linear ternary codes. Section 4 discuss the analysis of class DNA Golay code sub code. Final section 5\n  1553→concludes the paper with general remarks.\n  1554→II. E RROR CORRECTION FOR DNA STORAGE\n  1555→There are various error correction schemes used for DNA based data storage systems [11] [12] [13] but the effective schemes\n  1556→was first introduce by Church [6] and Goldman [7]. Using next generation synthesis and sequencing technology, Church came\n  1557→up with efficient one bit per base algorithm of encoding information bits into fix length of DNA chunks (99 bases). Flanking\n  1558→primers at the beginning and end of information data was inserted to identify the specific DNA segment in which the particular\n  1559→data was encoded. This method includes homo polymer repeated sequences that cause errors while writing and reading the\n  1560→\n  1561→=== 1502.05417v1.pdf ===\n  1562→The Evolution of Popular Music: USA 1960–2010\n  1563→Matthias Mauch,1∗ Robert M. MacCallum,2 Mark Levy,3 Armand M. Leroi2\n  1564→1\n  1565→\n  1566→arXiv:1502.05417v1 [physics.soc-ph] 17 Feb 2015\n  1567→\n  1568→School of Electronic Engineering and Computer Science, Queen Mary University of London, E1 4NS.\n  1569→United Kingdom, 2 Division of Life Sciences, Imperial College London, SW7 2AZ. United Kingdom,\n  1570→3\n  1571→Last.fm, 5–11 Lavingdon Street, London, SE1 0NZ. United Kingdom\n  1572→\n  1573→In modern societies, cultural change seems ceaseless. The flux of fashion is especially\n  1574→obvious for popular music. While much has been written about the origin and evolution\n  1575→of pop, most claims about its history are anecdotal rather than scientific in nature. To\n  1576→rectify this we investigate the US Billboard Hot 100 between 1960 and 2010. Using Music\n  1577→Information Retrieval (MIR) and text-mining tools we analyse the musical properties of\n  1578→∼17,000 recordings that appeared in the charts and demonstrate quantitative trends\n  1579→in their harmonic and timbral properties. We then use these properties to produce an\n  1580→audio-based classification of musical styles and study the evolution of musical diversity\n  1581→and disparity, testing, and rejecting, several classical theories of cultural change. Finally,\n  1582→we investigate whether pop musical evolution has been gradual or punctuated. We show\n  1583→that, although pop music has evolved continuously, it did so with particular rapidity during\n  1584→three stylistic “revolutions” around 1964, 1983 and 1991. We conclude by discussing\n  1585→how our study points the way to a quantitative science of cultural change.\n  1586→\n  1587→Introduction\n  1588→The history of popular music has long been debated by philosophers, sociologists, journalists and pop stars [1–6]. Their accounts, though rich in vivid musical lore and aesthetic\n  1589→judgements, lack what scientists want: rigorous tests of clear hypotheses based on quantitative data and statistics. Economics-minded social scientists studying the history of music\n  1590→have done better, but they are less interested in music than the means by which it is marketed [7–14]. The contrast with evolutionary biology—a historical science rich in quantitative\n  1591→data and models—is striking; the more so since cultural and organismic variety are both considered to be the result of modification-by-descent processes [15–18]. Indeed, linguists and\n  1592→archaeologists, studying the evolution of languages and material culture, commonly apply\n  1593→the same tools that evolutionary biologists do when studying the evolution of species [19–24].\n  1594→Inspired by their example, here we investigate the “fossil record” of American popular music.\n  1595→We adopt a diachronic, historical approach to ask several general questions: Has the variety\n  1596→of popular music increased or decreased over time? Is evolutionary change in popular music\n  1597→continuous or discontinuous? If discontinuous, when did the discontinuities occur?\n  1598→Our study rests on the recent availability of large collections of popular music with associated timestamps, and computational methods with which to measure them [25]. Analysis\n  1599→in traditional musicology and earlier data-driven ethnomusicology [26], while rich in structure [18], is slow and prone to inconsistencies and subjectivity. Promising diachronic studies\n  1600→on popular music exist, but they either lack scientific rigour [4], focus on technical aspects\n  1601→of audio such as loudness, vocabulary statistics and sequential complexity [25, 27], or are\n  1602→hampered by sample size [28]. The present work uniquely combines the power of a big,\n  1603→clearly defined diachronic dataset with the detailed examination of musically meaningful\n  1604→audio features.\n  1605→To delimit our sample, we focused on songs that appeared in the US Billboard Hot 100\n  1606→between 1960 and 2010. We obtained 30-second-long segments of 17,094 songs covering\n  1607→86% of the Hot 100, with a small bias towards missing songs in the earlier years. Since\n  1608→1\n  1609→\n  1610→\fAUDIO\n  1611→\n  1612→\n  1613→=== 1502.07298v1.pdf ===\n  1614→Trapped-ion Lissajous trajectories\n  1615→R. F. Rossetti, G. D. de Moraes Neto, J. Carlos Egues, and M. H. Y. Moussa1\n  1616→\n  1617→arXiv:1502.07298v1 [quant-ph] 25 Feb 2015\n  1618→\n  1619→1\n  1620→\n  1621→Instituto de Fı́sica de São Carlos, Universidade de São Paulo,\n  1622→Caixa Postal 369, 13560-970, São Carlos, São Paulo, Brazil\n  1623→\n  1624→Here we present a protocol for generating Lissajous curves with a trapped ion by engineering\n  1625→Rashba- and the Dresselhaus-type spin-orbit interactions in a Paul trap. The unique anisotropic\n  1626→Rashba αx , αy and Dresselhaus βx , βy couplings afforded by our setup also enables us to obtain\n  1627→an “unusual” Zitterbewegung, i.e., the semiconductor analog of the relativistic trembling motion\n  1628→of electrons, with cycloidal trajectories in the absence of magnetic fields. We have also introduced\n  1629→bounded SO interactions, confined to an upper-bound vibrational subspace of the Fock states, as an\n  1630→additional mechanism to manipulate the Lissajous motion of the trapped ion. Finally, we accounted\n  1631→for dissipative effects on the vibrational degrees of freedom of the ion and find that the Lissajous\n  1632→trajectories are still robust and well defined for realistic parameters.\n  1633→PACS numbers: 32.80.-t, 42.50.Ct, 42.50.Dv\n  1634→\n  1635→Increasing interest in quantum simulations and controllable trapped ion systems —used through the last two\n  1636→decades as a staging platform to investigate fundamental quantum phenomena [1] and to implement quantum\n  1637→information processing [2]— have led to the emulation\n  1638→of the quantum relativistic wave equation. Apart from\n  1639→trapped ions [3, 4], a number of distinct setups have been\n  1640→used to simulate zitterbewegung in a variety of physical\n  1641→systems such as quantum wells [5], photonic crystals [6],\n  1642→Bose-Einstein condensates [7] and ultracold atoms [8]. It\n  1643→has been demonstrated that Bose-Einstein condensates\n  1644→can provide an analog to sonic black holes [9] and the\n  1645→Higgs boson [10].\n  1646→In trapped-ion system Gerritsma et al. [11] reported\n  1647→a proof-of-principle quantum simulation of the onedimensional Dirac equation within a trapped-ion experiment. Measuring the time-evolving position of a single\n  1648→trapped ion set to mathematically simulate a free relativistic quantum particle, they demonstrated the Dirac\n  1649→Zitterbewegung, as anticipated in Ref. [3], for distinct\n  1650→initial superpositions of positive- and negative-energy\n  1651→spinor-like states. The momentum-spin coupling, which\n  1652→naturally takes place in the relativistic quantum regime,\n  1653→was simulated by the laser-induced coupling between the\n  1654→vibrational and the electronic states of the ion.\n  1655→Motivated by the exciting results in Refs. [3, 11], here\n  1656→we consider a single two-level atom in a two-dimensional\n  1657→Paul trap with four electrodes, Fig.1, and show how to\n  1658→obtain spin-orbit interactions of the Rashba and Dresselhaus types, ubiquitous in quantum spintronics and in\n  1659→emerging areas of condensed matter physics such as topological insulators and Majorana fermions. Our setup enables us to engineer Hamiltonians with anisotropic SO\n  1660→couplings such as\n  1661→HSO =\n  1662→\n  1663→αy\n  1664→\n  1665→=== 1511.08445v1.pdf ===\n  1666→arXiv:1511.08445v1 [q-bio.QM] 26 Nov 2015\n  1667→\n  1668→Characterization of DNA methylation as a\n  1669→function of biological complexity via\n  1670→dinucleotide inter-distances\n  1671→Giulia Paci1 , Giampaolo Cristadoro3 , Barbara Monti4 ,\n  1672→Marco Lenci2,3 , Mirko Degli Esposti3 , Gastone Castellani1,2 and\n  1673→Daniel Remondini∗1,2\n  1674→1\n  1675→\n  1676→Department of Physics and Astronomy, University of Bologna,\n  1677→Viale B. Pichat 6/2, 40127 Bologna, Italy\n  1678→2\n  1679→INFN, Bologna Unit, Viale B. Pichat 6/2, 40127 Bologna, Italy\n  1680→3\n  1681→Department of Mathematics, University of Bologna,\n  1682→Piazza di Porta S. Donato 5, 40126 Bologna, Italy\n  1683→4\n  1684→Department of Pharmacy and Biotechnology, University of\n  1685→Bologna, Via S. Donato 15, 40127 Bologna, Italy\n  1686→\n  1687→Abstract\n  1688→We perform a statistical study of the distances between successive occurrencies of a given dinucleotide in the DNA sequence for a number of\n  1689→organisms of different complexity. Our analysis highlights peculiar features of the dinucleotide CG distribution in mammalian DNA, pointing\n  1690→towards a connection with the role of such dinucleotide in DNA methylation. While the CG distributions of mammals exhibit exponential tails\n  1691→with comparable parameters, the picture for the other organisms studied\n  1692→(e.g., fish, insects, bacteria and viruses) is more heterogeneous, possibly because in these organisms DNA methylation has different functional\n  1693→roles. Our analysis suggests that the distribution of the distances between\n  1694→dinucleotides CG provides useful insights in characterizing and classifying\n  1695→organisms in terms of methylation functionalities.\n  1696→\n  1697→1\n  1698→\n  1699→Introduction\n  1700→\n  1701→The statistical analysis of DNA coding and non-coding sequences has revealed structures and correlations that go beyond the extent of short-range\n  1702→models, for example uncovering scale-invariant properties of the sequence as a\n  1703→whole. Results in this direction date back to the early 80’s [42, 35, 43, 29, 34,\n  1704→10, 5, 3, 44]. Despite these early observations, the functional role and nature\n  1705→(if any) of such long-range correlations are still to be clarified [28, 23, 7, 9, 6].\n  1706→Other statistical measures on genetic sequences have also been investigated, like\n  1707→∗ Corresponding author: daniel.remondini@unibo.it\n  1708→\n  1709→1\n  1710→\n  1711→\fentropies [30, 40, 20] or return time statistics for specific oligonucleotides [8, 1].\n  1712→In particular, the first-return-time distribution proved to be a powerful tool\n  1713→to investigate the statistical properties of symbolic sequences in general. The\n  1714→idea dates back to the pioneering work of Poincaré [37], on the trajectories of\n  1715→bounded dynamical systems. Thereafter, much effort has been devoted to the\n  1716→\n  1717→=== 1607.00266v1.pdf ===\n  1718→1\n  1719→\n  1720→The Art of DNA Strings: Sixteen Years of DNA\n  1721→Coding Theory\n  1722→Dixita Limbachiya, Bansari Rao and Manish K. Gupta\n  1723→Dhirubhai Ambani Institute of Information and Communication Technology\n  1724→Gandhinagar, Gujarat, 382007 India\n  1725→Email:dlimbachiya@acm.org, bansari s rao@daiict.ac.in and mankg@computer.org\n  1726→\n  1727→arXiv:1607.00266v1 [cs.IT] 1 Jul 2016\n  1728→\n  1729→Abstract\n  1730→The idea of computing with DNA was given by Tom Head in 1987, however in 1994 in a seminal paper, the actual successful\n  1731→experiment for DNA computing was performed by Adleman. The heart of the DNA computing is the DNA hybridization, however,\n  1732→it is also the source of errors. Thus the success of the DNA computing depends on the error control techniques. The classical\n  1733→coding theory techniques have provided foundation for the current information and communication technology (ICT). Thus it\n  1734→is natural to expect that coding theory will be the foundational subject for the DNA computing paradigm. For the successful\n  1735→experiments with DNA computing usually we design DNA strings which are sufficiently dissimilar. This leads to the construction\n  1736→of a large set of DNA strings which satisfy certain combinatorial and thermodynamic constraints. Over the last 16 years, many\n  1737→approaches such as combinatorial, algebraic, computational have been used to construct such DNA strings. In this work, we survey\n  1738→this interesting area of DNA coding theory by providing key ideas of the area and current known results.\n  1739→\n  1740→I. I NTRODUCTION\n  1741→Information and Communication Technology (ICT) has come a long way in the last 60\n  1742→years. We have now connected world of devices talking to each other or performing the\n  1743→computation. This is possible due to the advancement of coding and information theory. In\n  1744→1994, two new directions in ICT emerged viz. quantum computing and DNA computing.\n  1745→The heart of any computing or communications is coding theory. Thus two new directions\n  1746→in coding theory also emerged such as quantum coding theory and DNA coding theory. This\n  1747→paper focuses on the later area giving a comprehensive overview of techniques and challenges\n  1748→of DNA coding theory from last 16 years. In 1994, L.Adleman performed the computation\n  1749→using DNA strands to solve an instance of the Hamiltonian path problem giving birth to\n  1750→Figure 1: DNA structure with\n  1751→DNA computing [1]–[4]. DNA is a double strand built by the pairing the four basic building its four nucleotides A-Adenine,\n  1752→units A-(Adenine), C-(Cytosine), G-(Guanine), T-(Thymine) which are called nucleotides. G-Guanine, C-Cytosine and T The DNA strand is held by the important feature called complementary base pairing which Thymine. These are the basic buildconnects the Watson Crick complementary bases with each other denoted by AC = T and ing blocks of DNA which are held\n  1753→GC = C. The backbone of DNA is an alternating chains of sugar and phosphate. DNA is by Hydrogen bonds in a double\n  1754→helical manner. Each DNA base\n  1755→an ideal source of computing due to its stable, dense and its self replicating property [5]. is paired with the complementary\n  1756→After the successful experiment by Adleman, area of DNA computing [6] flourished into bases such that the yellow band is\n  1757→different directions like DNA tile assembly [7] [8], building of DNA nano-structures [9] A which is connected to its comple[10], studying error correcting properties in DNA sequences [11] [12] and DNA based data mentary base T (green band) while\n  1758→storage system [13]. But it was only with the identification of mathematical properties in C (blue band) is paired with red G\n  1759→(red band)\n  1760→DNA sequences [14] [15], it inspired many researchers to explore this new amalgamation of\n  1761→biology and coding theory [16]. DNA computing [17] promises to solve many NP-complete\n  1762→problems like Hamilton path problem, satisfiability problem (SAT) [18] and many others with massive parallelization [19]. To\n  1763→perform computation using DNA strands, a specific set of DNA sequences are required with particular properties. Such set of\n  1764→DNA strands satisfying various constraints [20], [21] play an important role in biomolecular computation [22] [23].\n  1765→The aim of this manuscript is to elucidate the current state of art of DNA codes as shown in Figure 2, DNA codes constraints\n  1766→and especially the new methods contributing to construct DNA codes from algebraic coding. It summarizes the research on\n  1767→DNA codes which may help the researcher to get a broad picture of the area. Also, it gives brief overview on the applications\n  1768→\n  1769→=== 1609.05333v2.pdf ===\n  1770→Like-charge attraction and opposite-charge decomplexation between polymers and\n  1771→DNA molecules\n  1772→Sahin Buyukdagli\n  1773→\n  1774→arXiv:1609.05333v2 [cond-mat.soft] 14 Feb 2017\n  1775→\n  1776→Department of Physics, Bilkent University, Ankara 06800, Turkey\n  1777→(Dated: January 4, 2018)\n  1778→We scrutinize the effect of polyvalent ions on polymer-DNA interactions. We extend a recently\n  1779→developed test charge theory [S. Buyukdagli et al., Phys. Rev. E 94, 042502 (2016)] to the case of\n  1780→a stiff polymer interacting with a DNA molecule in an electrolyte mixture. The theory accounts for\n  1781→one-loop level electrostatic correlation effects such as the ionic cloud deformation around the strongly\n  1782→charged DNA molecule as well as image-charge forces induced by the low DNA permittivity. Our\n  1783→model can reproduce and explain various characteristics of the experimental phase diagrams for\n  1784→polymer solutions. First, the addition of polyvalent cations to the electrolyte solution results in\n  1785→the attraction of the negatively charged polymer by the DNA molecule. The glue of the like-charge\n  1786→attraction is the enhanced shielding of the polymer charges by the dense counterion layer at the\n  1787→DNA surface. Secondly, through the shielding of the DNA-induced electrostatic potential, mono- and\n  1788→polyvalent cations of large concentration both suppress the like-charge attraction. Within the same\n  1789→formalism, we also predict a new opposite-charge repulsion effect between the DNA molecule and a\n  1790→positively charged polymer. In the presence of polyvalent anions such as sulfate or phosphate, their\n  1791→repulsion by the DNA charges leads to the charge screening deficiency of the region around the DNA\n  1792→molecule. This translates into a repulsive force that results in the decomplexation of the polymer\n  1793→from DNA. This opposite-charge repulsion phenomenon can be verified by current experiments and\n  1794→the underlying mechanism can be beneficial to gene therapeutic applications where the control over\n  1795→polymer-DNA interactions is the key factor.\n  1796→PACS numbers: 05.20.Jj,82.45.Gj,82.35.Rs\n  1797→\n  1798→I.\n  1799→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"a50557a3-082e-47bf-8463-1e5cb58bdacb","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:03:38.013Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c98a790f-f05f-47f6-a29d-b542763fbf7e"}
{"parentUuid":"a50557a3-082e-47bf-8463-1e5cb58bdacb","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a5de01b","slug":"lovely-wishing-lantern","type":"progress","data":{"type":"hook_progress","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","hookName":"PostToolUse:Read","command":"callback"},"parentToolUseID":"toolu_vrtx_017wCHQSX67MF9kpEtRbtnYE","toolUseID":"toolu_vrtx_017wCHQSX67MF9kpEtRbtnYE","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:03:38.013Z","uuid":"98d7b9d5-6566-419b-ad57-c71c2629294f"}
{"parentUuid":"98d7b9d5-6566-419b-ad57-c71c2629294f","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a5de01b","slug":"lovely-wishing-lantern","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_vrtx_01BYERKmD36hnKAZZXQNy4xB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01PdxKEMZmqhnSzUaXWsvaTb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-root/2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_017qp5RfQsP2SwYRFpmmRwc6.txt","offset":1800,"limit":600}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12109,"cache_read_input_tokens":88740,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12109,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXb5rj7NkfGYckRfyvFCs","type":"assistant","uuid":"df8caf54-0fa0-4bed-a3a5-140e57363b07","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:03:43.592Z"}
{"parentUuid":"df8caf54-0fa0-4bed-a3a5-140e57363b07","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2d9ff8ec-9536-4d8e-bf50-c694be83d0f4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a5de01b","slug":"lovely-wishing-lantern","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01PdxKEMZmqhnSzUaXWsvaTb","type":"tool_result","content":"  1800→INTRODUCTION\n  1801→\n  1802→DNA is the central building block of life. The biological functions of this anionic molecule are intimately\n  1803→related to its electrostatic coupling with the surrounding\n  1804→macromolecular charges. In this context, the like-charge\n  1805→attraction phenomenon plays a capital role in DNA packing around histones [1], the condensation of polymer solutions [2–9], DNA adsorption on anionic substrates [10–\n  1806→13], and gene delivery techniques [14]. The characterization of like-charge attraction is thus of paramount importance for understanding and controlling in-vivo and\n  1807→in-vitro biological processes involving DNA molecules.\n  1808→On the experimental side, it is well established that\n  1809→polyvalent counterions are the essential ingredient of likecharge polymer attraction [15–23]. More precisely, these\n  1810→experiments showed that the occurrence of like-charged\n  1811→polymer condensation at physiological salt densities necessitates the presence of trivalent counterions in the\n  1812→electrolyte solution. It should be noted that the polyvalent cation-induced like-charge attraction cannot be\n  1813→explained from the perspective of mean-field (MF) electrostatics; regardless of the electrolyte composition and\n  1814→the ionic valency, the MF theory always predicts repulsion between similar charges. In order to interpret\n  1815→this seemingly counterintuitive phenomenon, a beyondMF formulation of the problem including charge correlations is needed. In this direction, previous theories\n  1816→have considered various mechanisms such as counterion\n  1817→binding [5, 6, 23–28], attractive van der Waals interactions [29], and Wigner crystal formation occurring in\n  1818→\n  1819→the electrostatic strong-coupling regime [30, 31]. More\n  1820→\n  1821→=== 1708.07404v3.pdf ===\n  1822→Mass of Schwarzschild Black Holes Is Indeed Zero And Black Hole Candidates Are\n  1823→Quasi Black Holes\n  1824→Abhas Mitra∗\n  1825→\n  1826→arXiv:1708.07404v3 [physics.gen-ph] 10 May 2018\n  1827→\n  1828→Homi Bhabha National Institute, Mumbai-400094, India\n  1829→(Dated: May 11, 2018)\n  1830→A Schwarzschild Black Hole (BH) is the gravitational field due to a neutral point mass, and it\n  1831→turns out that the gravitational mass of a neutral point mass: M = 0 (Arnowitt, Deser, Misner, PRL\n  1832→4, 375, 1960). The same result is also suggested by Janis, Newman, and Winicour (PRL 20, 878,\n  1833→1968). In 1969, Bel gave an explicit proof that for a Schwarzschild BH, M = 0 (Bel, JMP 10, 1051,\n  1834→1969). The same result follows from the fact the timelike geodesic of a test particle would turn null\n  1835→if it would ever arrive at an event horizon (Mitra, FPL, 2000, 2002). Non-occurrrence of trapped\n  1836→surfaces in continued gravitational collapse too demands M = 0 for black hole (Mitra, Pramana,\n  1837→73, 615, 2009). Physically, for a point mass at R = 0, one expects Ric ∼ M δ(R = 0) (Narlikar &\n  1838→Padmanabhan, Found. Phys., 18, 659, 1988). But the black hole solution is obtained from Ric = 0.\n  1839→Again this is the most direct proof that M = 0 for a Schwarzschild black hole. Implication of\n  1840→this result is that the observed massive black hole candidates are non-singular quasi black holes or\n  1841→black hole mimickers which can possess strong magnetic fields as has been observed. The echoes\n  1842→from LIGO signals, if true, may be the direct evidence that the pertinent compact objects are BH\n  1843→mimickers and not true vacuum BHs.\n  1844→INTRODUCTION\n  1845→\n  1846→Starting from Einstein, most of the founding fathers of general relativity thought that though the vacuum\n  1847→Schwarzschild equation is correct, its interpretation in terms of black holes is incorrect. Essentially, they pleaded\n  1848→that while a massive star can contract almost upto its Schwarzschild surface, it cannot contract upto the redline\n  1849→R = Rs = 2M (G = c = 1), and its radius R > 2M . Even when the mathematical BH paradigm got well established\n  1850→by early 1960s, Dirac ruled out the concept of point singularities [1]:\n  1851→“The mathematicians can go beyond this Schwarzschild radius, and get inside, but I would maintain that this inside\n  1852→region is not physical space, because to send a signal inside and get it out again would take an infinite time, so I\n  1853→feel that the space inside the Schwarzschild radius must belong to a different universe and should not be taken into\n  1854→account in any physical theory. So from the physical point of view, the possibility of having a point singularity in the\n  1855→Einstein field is ruled out. Each particle must have a finite size no smaller than the Schwarzschild radius.”\n  1856→From physical perspective, contrary to the myth, BH paradigm got never established because the physical nature\n  1857→of BH interiors have never been understood. This becomes clear from the following admission by three physicists who\n  1858→believe in the BH paradigm. These authors commented about the coordinate transformations which endeavour to\n  1859→present the BH interior as regular region of spacetime [2]:\n  1860→The solutions that do away with the interior singularity and the event horizon, although interesting in themselves,\n  1861→sweep the inherent conceptual difficulties of black holes under the rug. In concluding, we note that the interior\n  1862→structure of realistic black holes have not been satisfactorily determined, and are still open to considerable debate.\n  1863→And the reason that the BH interior is not understood after more than a century of the discovery of the Schwarzschild\n  1864→(actually Droste -Hilbert) solution is that there is no true BH, and no BH interior. In other words true mathematical\n  1865→Schwarzschild BH has M = 0, and the so-called BHs are only BH mimickers or quasi-BHs. While we established this\n  1866→result from various independent lines of aruments since 2000, in 2009, we offered a more direct proof [3]. This proof\n  1867→was based on the 170 year old rule of multivariate calculus first obtained by Carl Jacobi (1804 -51 CE): coordinate\n  1868→transformation from say from x, y to u(x, y), v(x, y), obeys the rule\n  1869→dxdy = dydx = Jdudv = Jdvdu\n  1870→\n  1871→(1)\n  1872→\n  1873→=== 1712.05683v3.pdf ===\n  1874→Quantum simulation of the Weyl equation with a trapped ion\n  1875→De-Sheng Li,1, ∗ Chun-Wang Wu,1, † Lin-Ze He,1, ‡ Wei Wu,1, § and Ping-Xing Chen1, ¶\n  1876→1\n  1877→Interdisciplinary Center for Quantum Information,\n  1878→National University of Defense Technology, Changsha 410073, P.R.China\n  1879→\n  1880→arXiv:1712.05683v3 [quant-ph] 4 Apr 2019\n  1881→\n  1882→The Weyl equation describes chiral massless relativistic particles, called Weyl fermions, which\n  1883→have important relations to neutrinos. A direct observation of the dynamics of Weyl fermions in an\n  1884→experiment is difficult to achieve. This study investigates a method of simulating the Weyl equation\n  1885→in 1+2-dimension by a single trapped ion. The predictions about a two-dimensional Zitterbewegung\n  1886→and an especially interesting phenomenon of Weyl fermions can be tested by the future trapped ion\n  1887→experiment, which might enhance our understanding of neutrinos.\n  1888→\n  1889→INTRODUCTION\n  1890→\n  1891→The Weyl equation can be derived from the Dirac equation as rest mass equals zero. Neutrinos have a nonzero\n  1892→tiny mass; hence, the Weyl equation can be used to approximately describe neutrinos. In the Standard Model\n  1893→of particle physics (SM), neutrinos are assumed to be\n  1894→massless, and only left-handed neutrinos exist [1]. The\n  1895→following remains an open question: why do left-handed\n  1896→neutrinos exist, but not right-handed neutrinos? Neutrinos do not have an electric charge, and are difficult\n  1897→to detect. Moreover, its properties are still not exactly\n  1898→clear. An analog quantum simulation [2–6] of the Weyl\n  1899→equation in a trapped ion system might give us a new perspective to understand neutrinos and the electro-weak\n  1900→interaction. Analog quantum simulators aim at using\n  1901→controllable systems to simulate systems that are hard\n  1902→to access in an experiment. Some examples of the analog quantum simulation are as follows: a black hole is\n  1903→simulated in Bose–Einstein condensation by sonic gravitation correspondence [7]; the Klein paradox is simulated using two ions [8]; the Majorana equation and unphysical operations are simulated using a trapped ion\n  1904→[9]; and quantum field theory is simulated using trapped\n  1905→ions[10, 11]. The Zitterbewegung phenomenon, which is\n  1906→a trembling motion for quantum-relativistic particles, including Weyl fermions, has been widely discussed in the\n  1907→recent years[12–17]. For example, the theoretical proposal and the experimental result of the quantum simulation of the Dirac equation and Zitterbewegung have\n  1908→been provided by [12, 13]. The neutrino’s Zitterbewegung\n  1909→has been discussed by [14].\n  1910→We present herein a method of simulating the 1 +\n  1911→2-dimensional Weyl equation using the analog quantum simulator. The two-dimension Zitterbewegung of\n  1912→Weyl fermions can be tested by the trapped ion experiment. Another interesting phenomenon that can\n  1913→be tested through the experiment is the axisymmetric\n  1914→Weyl fermion initial state that will evolve into a nonaxisymmetric state. We show that the Weyl equation\n  1915→evolution can be simulated in a single trapped ion using\n  1916→red- and blue-sideband (bichromatic) laser beams from\n  1917→the x- and y-directions, respectively. The initial state\n  1918→\n  1919→is prepared by Doppler cooling, sideband cooling, and\n  1920→displacement Hamiltonian. The average momentum of\n  1921→the simulated particle is adjusted by the displacement\n  1922→Hamiltonian. We can use the wave function reconstruction method [13] to obtain the average position and possibility distribution at a certain time t by measuring the\n  1923→excited state’s population.\n  1924→\n  1925→=== 1804.04839v1.pdf ===\n  1926→Classification of large DNA methylation datasets for identifying cancer drivers\n  1927→Fabrizio Cellia, Fabio Cumboa,b, and Emanuel Weitschekc,a\n  1928→a\n  1929→\n  1930→Institute of Systems Analysis and Computer Science, National Research Council, Via dei Taurini 19, 00185 Rome, Italy\n  1931→\n  1932→b\n  1933→\n  1934→Department of Engineering, Roma Tre University, Via della Vasca Navale 79, 00154 Rome, Italy\n  1935→\n  1936→c\n  1937→\n  1938→Department of Engineering, Uninettuno International University. Corso Vittorio Emanuele II, 39 00186 Rome, Italy\n  1939→\n  1940→Abstract\n  1941→DNA methylation is a well-studied genetic modification crucial to regulate the functioning of the genome. Its alterations play\n  1942→an important role in tumorigenesis and tumor-suppression. Thus, studying DNA methylation data may help biomarker\n  1943→discovery in cancer. Since public data on DNA methylation become abundant – and considering the high number of\n  1944→methylated sites (features) present in the genome – it is important to have a method for efficiently processing such large\n  1945→datasets. Relying on big data technologies, we propose BIGBIOCL an algorithm that can apply supervised classification\n  1946→methods to datasets with hundreds of thousands of features. It is designed for the extraction of alternative and equivalent\n  1947→classification models through iterative deletion of selected features.\n  1948→We run experiments on DNA methylation datasets extracted from The Cancer Genome Atlas, focusing on three tumor types:\n  1949→breast, kidney, and thyroid carcinomas. We perform classifications extracting several methylated sites and their associated\n  1950→genes with accurate performance (accuracy>97%). Results suggest that BIGBIOCL can perform hundreds of classification\n  1951→iterations on hundreds of thousands of features in few hours. Moreover, we compare the performance of our method with\n  1952→other state-of-the-art classifiers and with a wide-spread DNA methylation analysis method based on network analysis.\n  1953→Finally, we are able to efficiently compute multiple alternative classification models and extract - from DNA-methylation\n  1954→large datasets - a set of candidate genes to be further investigated to determine their active role in cancer. BIGBIOCL, results\n  1955→of experiments, and a guide to carry on new experiments are freely available on GitHub at\n  1956→https://github.com/fcproj/BIGBIOCL.\n  1957→\n  1958→Keywords: classification; machine learning; DNA methylation; cancer; disease diagnostic predictive models; algorithms and techniques to\n  1959→speed up the analysis of big medical data.\n  1960→\n  1961→\f1\n  1962→\n  1963→Introduction\n  1964→\n  1965→Tumor, or neoplasm, is a mass of tissue originated from an abnormal and\n  1966→uncontrolled division of eukaryotic cells. When tumoral cells invade and\n  1967→destroy surrounding tissues, the tumor is malignant and it is called\n  1968→cancer.\n  1969→According\n  1970→to\n  1971→the\n  1972→World\n  1973→Health\n  1974→Organization\n  1975→(http://www.who.int/mediacentre/factsheets/fs297/en/), nearly one six of\n  1976→\n  1977→=== 1807.00924v2.pdf ===\n  1978→Trapped Ion Quantum Information Processing with Squeezed Phonons\n  1979→Wenchao Ge,1, 2 Brian C. Sawyer,3 Joseph W. Britton,1 Kurt Jacobs,1, 4, 5 John J. Bollinger,6 and Michael Foss-Feig1, 7, 8\n  1980→\n  1981→arXiv:1807.00924v2 [quant-ph] 5 Jan 2019\n  1982→\n  1983→2\n  1984→\n  1985→1\n  1986→United States Army Research Laboratory, Adelphi, Maryland 20783, USA\n  1987→The Institute for Research in Electronics and Applied Physics (IREAP), College Park, Maryland 20740, USA\n  1988→3\n  1989→Georgia Tech Research Institute, Atlanta, Georgia 30332, USA\n  1990→4\n  1991→Department of Physics, University of Massachusetts at Boston, Boston, Massachusetts 02125, USA\n  1992→5\n  1993→Hearne Institute for Theoretical Physics, Louisiana State University, Baton Rouge, Louisiana 70803, USA\n  1994→6\n  1995→National Institute of Standards and Technology, Boulder, Colorado 80305, USA,\n  1996→7\n  1997→Joint Quantum Institute, NIST/University of Maryland, College Park, Maryland 20742, USA\n  1998→8\n  1999→Joint Center for Quantum Information and Computer Science,\n  2000→NIST/University of Maryland, College Park, Maryland 20742, USA\n  2001→\n  2002→Trapped ions offer a pristine platform for quantum computation and simulation, but improving their coherence\n  2003→remains a crucial challenge. Here, we propose and analyze a new strategy to enhance the coherent interactions\n  2004→in trapped ion systems via parametric amplification of the ions’ motion—by squeezing the collective motional\n  2005→modes (phonons), the spin-spin interactions they mediate can be significantly enhanced. We illustrate the power\n  2006→of this approach by showing how it can enhance collective spin states useful for quantum metrology, and how\n  2007→it can improve the speed and fidelity of two-qubit gates in multi-ion systems, important ingredients for scalable\n  2008→trapped ion quantum computation. Our results are also directly relevant to numerous other physical platforms\n  2009→in which spin interactions are mediated by bosons.\n  2010→\n  2011→Trapped ions are among the best developed implementations of numerous quantum technologies, including quantum\n  2012→computers [1], quantum simulators [2], and quantum measurement devices [3]. For example, universal quantum gate\n  2013→sets have been implemented with extremely high fidelity in\n  2014→small systems [4, 5], while quantum spin dynamics and entanglement generation have been demonstrated among tens\n  2015→[6] and even hundreds [7] of ions. For all of these applications, the general approach is to identify a qubit, i.e., two\n  2016→metastable atomic states, and then engineer interactions between qubits by controllably coupling them to the ions’ collective motion (phonons), typically using lasers [1, 8] or magnetic field gradients [9, 10]. Putting aside the details of what\n  2017→specifically constitutes a qubit (hyperfine states of an ion, Rydberg levels of a neutral atom, charge states of a superconducting circuit), and what type of boson mediates interactions\n  2018→between them (phonons or photons), this basic paradigm of\n  2019→controllable boson-mediated interactions between qubits is at\n  2020→the heart of many physical implementations of quantum technologies. In all such systems, a key technical challenge is to\n  2021→make the interactions as strong as possible without compromising the qubit.\n  2022→For trapped ions, the strength of interactions between qubits\n  2023→(from here forward called spins) is often limited by the available laser power or by the current that can be driven through\n  2024→a thin trap electrode. Where these technical limitations can be\n  2025→overcome, other more fundamental limits remain. For example, the scattering due to the laser beams that generate spinspin interactions can be the dominant source of decoherence\n  2026→[4, 5, 7], in which case using more laser power is not necessarily helpful [11–13]. Moreover, in many-ion strings larger laser\n  2027→power can lead to decoherence through off-resonant coupling\n  2028→\n  2029→=== 1808.04531v1.pdf ===\n  2030→Perseus in Sicily: from black hole to cluster outskirts\n  2031→Proceedings IAU Symposium No. 342, 2018\n  2032→Keiichi Asada, Elisabete de Gouveia dal Pino,\n  2033→Hiroshi Nagai, Rodrigo Nemmen, Marcello Giroletti, eds.\n  2034→\n  2035→c 2018 International Astronomical Union\n  2036→DOI: 00.0000/X000000000000000X\n  2037→\n  2038→Black hole demographics from TDE\n  2039→modeling\n  2040→T. Mageshwaran1 and A. Mangalam2\n  2041→\n  2042→arXiv:1808.04531v1 [astro-ph.HE] 14 Aug 2018\n  2043→\n  2044→Indian Institute of Astrophysics, Bangalore, India\n  2045→email: 1 mageshwaran@iiap.res.in, 2 mangalam@iiap.res.in\n  2046→Abstract.\n  2047→The occurence rate of tidal disruption events (TDEs) by survey missions depend on the\n  2048→black hole mass function of the galaxies, properties of the stellar cusp and mass of the central\n  2049→black hole. Using a power law density profile with Kroupa mass function, we solve the steady\n  2050→state Fokker-Planck to calculate the theoretical capture rate of stars by the black hole. Using a\n  2051→steady accretion model, the Schechter black hole mass function (BHMF) and the cosmological\n  2052→parameters, we calculate the detection rate of TDEs for various surveys which is then fit with the\n  2053→observed TDE rates to extract the Schechter parameters. The rate tension between observation\n  2054→(∼ 10−5 yr−1 ) and theory (∼ 10−4 yr−1 ) for individual galaxies is explained by the statistical\n  2055→average over the BHMF.\n  2056→Keywords. galaxies: nuclei, galaxies: luminosity function, mass function, Galaxy: kinematics\n  2057→and dynamics, galaxies: statistics\n  2058→\n  2059→1. Introduction\n  2060→A star orbiting close to the black hole such that the black hole’s tidal gravity exceeds the\n  2061→star’s self-gravity is tidally disrupted at the pericenter rp ⩽ rt , where rt = (M• /M⋆ )1/3 R⋆\n  2062→is the tidal radius and is called as tidal disruption events (TDEs) [Rees 1998]. The physical\n  2063→parameters crucial for the study of TDEs are the black hole (BH) mass M• , specific\n  2064→orbital energy E and angular momentum J, star mass M⋆ and radius R⋆ . For a stellar\n  2065→density ρ(r) ∝ r−γ in the galactic center and the mass function ξ(m) given by Kroupa\n  2066→(2001), Mageshwaran & Mangalam (2015) (hereafter MM15) solved the steady state\n  2067→Fokker-Planck equation (Merritt 2013) to obtain the capture rate Ṅt ∝ M•−0.3 . We use\n  2068→TDEs as a probe to derive the BHMF from observed detection rate for various surveys.\n  2069→Milosavljević et al. (2006) have showed that TDEs makes a negligible contribution at\n  2070→the higher end of luminosity functions. Stone & Metzger (2016) using the Schechter\n  2071→luminosity function showed that the volumetric rate of TDE detection is sensitive to the\n  2072→occupation fraction of low-mass black holes. Van Velzen (2018) using optical/UV selected\n  2073→TDEs and forward modeling showed that luminosity function ∝ L−2.5 .\n  2074→\n  2075→2. Black hole mass function\n  2076→We consider a separable form of BHMF Φ(M• , z) = µ(M• )X(z), where X(z) = 1−δ(z)\n  2077→with duty cycle δ(z) = 10−3 (z/0.1)2.5 [Chen et al. 2007] and µ(M• ) obtained using\n  2078→Schechter luminosity function Φ(LR ) = Φ⋆ (LR /L⋆ )−α e−LR /L⋆ , where LR is the R-band\n  2079→1/n\n  2080→\n  2081→=== 1901.01045v1.pdf ===\n  2082→arXiv:1901.01045v1 [physics.pop-ph] 4 Jan 2019\n  2083→\n  2084→Black hole essay\n  2085→\n  2086→Enrico Brehm\n  2087→Max Planck Institute for Gravitational Physics\n  2088→Albert Einstein Institute\n  2089→Potsdam-Golm, D-14476, Germany\n  2090→\n  2091→E-mail: brehm@aei.mpg.de\n  2092→Abstract: This essay gives a very general introduction to Schwarzschild black holes. First,\n  2093→it focuses on some of its classical features as solutions to Einstein’s theory of gravity. In the\n  2094→second part it discusses briefly some specific quantum aspects and how a black hole processes quantum information. No previous knowledge about black holes, gravity or quantum\n  2095→mechanics is required.\n  2096→\n  2097→\f1\n  2098→\n  2099→The classical viewpoint\n  2100→\n  2101→Black holes are some of the strangest phenomenons in our univers. Here, we first want\n  2102→to discuss them as objects of a classical physical theory, where classical means that we\n  2103→forget about all possible quantum effects and their consequences. In the present case the\n  2104→underlying classical theory is Einstein’s theory of gravity [1]. It describes space and time in\n  2105→terms of a collection of fields whose behaviour is dictated by the Einstein equations.\n  2106→One natural question to ask is how does this theory describe space and time around a\n  2107→massive spherical object like a star? The solution to this was found by Schwarzschild [4].\n  2108→It is valid all around any static round object and, remarkably, only depends on its mass.\n  2109→However, quite strange things can happen when all that mass is packed within a specific\n  2110→radius named after Schwarzschild. Then a so-called event horizon forms at the Schwarzschild\n  2111→radius and we start talking about a black hole.\n  2112→Before we shed more light on the strangeness of black holes, let us try to get some intuition for the circumstances under which black holes can appear. The Schwarzschild radius\n  2113→rs and the mass M of a spherical object have a very easy relation: they are proportional\n  2114→to each other, rs = a · M , with a being very small when we measure in standard units.\n  2115→Let us for example consider an object with the mass of earth, then its Schwarzschild radius\n  2116→is given by only about 9 mm! No physical process is known in which all of earth can be\n  2117→compressed that much and it seams quite unlikely that there are many (if at all) black holes\n  2118→with the mass of earth. The situation changes when we consider bigger and bigger masses.\n  2119→This is because the volume within the Schwarzschild radius, i.e., the space where we can\n  2120→store all the mass to form a black hole, increases much faster. In fact, if we double the mass\n  2121→we get roughly eight times more volume to store it. The formation of black holes becames\n  2122→easier the heavier they are. It is known that there are mechanisms at the end of the lifetime\n  2123→of very massive stars that lead to the formation of so-called stellar black holes [3]. Even\n  2124→heavier black holes can for example develop when stellar ones merge.\n  2125→Let us return to the promised strangeness of black holes. Actually, if we are far away\n  2126→from the black hole, it is not much different from any other stellar object of the same mass.\n  2127→The only big difference is that we do not see any light originating from the black hole. An\n  2128→interesting effect, that occurs when we come closer to it, is that time for us passes slower\n  2129→than for those who stay away from the black hole. However, the effect is not tractable in a\n  2130→direct way. Any clock that we carry with us behaves completely fine from our perspective.\n  2131→Only if we return back to a place far away from the black hole and compare the times\n  2132→\n  2133→=== 1901.01149v1.pdf ===\n  2134→arXiv:1901.01149v1 [physics.pop-ph] 2 Jan 2019\n  2135→\n  2136→Black holes and quantum entanglement\n  2137→\n  2138→Ro Jefferson\n  2139→Max Planck Institute for Gravitational Physics (Albert Einstein Institute),\n  2140→Am Mühlenberg 1, D-14476 Potsdam-Golm, Germany\n  2141→\n  2142→E-mail: rjefferson@aei.mpg.de\n  2143→Abstract: The black hole information paradox is a contradiction between fundamental\n  2144→principles which has puzzled physicists for over forty years. The crux of the problem lies in\n  2145→an assumption about the structure of entanglement across the event horizon, namely, that the\n  2146→Hilbert space factorizes. While valid in quantum mechanics, this fails drastically in quantum\n  2147→field theory, and hence a deeper understanding of entanglement is required if further progress\n  2148→is to be made. Recently, ideas from algebraic quantum field theory have provided new insight\n  2149→into this issue, and show promise for elucidating the connection between entropy and horizons\n  2150→that underlies black hole thermodynamics.\n  2151→\n  2152→Essay written for the Black Hole Initiative’s 2018 paper competition.\n  2153→\n  2154→\fIn 1973, Jacob Bekenstein observed [1] that black holes must be endowed with an entropy\n  2155→in order to preserve the second law of thermodynamics; otherwise, one could decrease the\n  2156→entropy of the universe by simply throwing subsystems with high entropy into a black hole.\n  2157→At face value, this is an intuitive proposal: since the information about the degrees of freedom\n  2158→that comprise the hypothetical subsystem would then be hidden behind the event horizon, it\n  2159→makes sense to count them among the microstates of the black hole.\n  2160→The unintuitive twist comes from the realization that this naı̈ve bookkeeping is not at\n  2161→all how black holes operate. The entropy of familiar systems scales with the volume thereof,\n  2162→which is consistent with simply counting the constituent (e.g., particulate) degrees of freedom.\n  2163→Black hole entropy, in stark contrast, scales with the area of the event horizon. Bekenstein’s\n  2164→original motivation for this claim hinged largely on Stephen Hawking’s 1971 result [2] that\n  2165→the surface area of a black hole cannot decrease in any classical process (the so-called “area\n  2166→theorem”). This lead Bekenstein to propose an analogy between black holes and statistical\n  2167→thermodynamics, which has since been enshrined in the laws of black hole thermodynamics.\n  2168→Classically however, black holes do not radiate (hence the name), and therefore have\n  2169→zero temperature and consequently zero thermodynamic entropy. For this reason, black hole\n  2170→thermodynamics initially appeared purely epistemic—a reflection of our knowledge of reality,\n  2171→rather than the real (or ontic) nature thereof. But this metaphysical state of affairs soon\n  2172→changed when Hawking showed [3] that, quantum mechanically, black holes do radiate, with\n  2173→characteristic temperature\n  2174→1\n  2175→,\n  2176→(0.1)\n  2177→T =\n  2178→8πM\n  2179→and entropy\n  2180→A\n  2181→S=\n  2182→,\n  2183→(0.2)\n  2184→\n  2185→=== 1902.00206v1.pdf ===\n  2186→INTRODUCTION\n  2187→\n  2188→Quantum Thermodynamics book\n  2189→\n  2190→Probing Quantum Fluctuations of Work with a Trapped Ion\n  2191→Yao Lu,1, ∗ Shuoming An,1, † Jing-Ning Zhang,1, ‡ and Kihwan Kim1, §\n  2192→1\n  2193→\n  2194→Center for Quantum Information, Institute for Interdisciplinary Information Sciences,\n  2195→Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, P. R. China\n  2196→(Dated: February 4, 2019)\n  2197→\n  2198→arXiv:1902.00206v1 [quant-ph] 1 Feb 2019\n  2199→\n  2200→In this chapter, we illustrate how a trapped ion system can be used for the experimental study of\n  2201→quantum thermodynamics, in particular, quantum fluctuation of work. As technology of nano/micro\n  2202→scale develops, it becomes critical to understand thermodynamics at the quantum mechanical level.\n  2203→The trapped ion system is a representative physical platform to experimentally demonstrate quantum phenomena with excellent control and precision. We provide a basic introduction of the trapped\n  2204→ion system and present the theoretical framework for the experimental study of quantum thermodynamics. Then we bring out two concrete examples of the experimental demonstrations. Finally,\n  2205→we discuss the results and the future of the experimental study of quantum thermodynamics with\n  2206→trapped ion systems.\n  2207→\n  2208→INTRODUCTION\n  2209→\n  2210→The technologies of nano/micro scale have been rapidly developed [1–3]. On the nano/micro scale,\n  2211→quantum fluctuations become important and such quantum effects should be fully taken into account\n  2212→to properly understand and manipulate nano/micro systems [1–9]. The extension of the principles of\n  2213→thermodynamics [10, 11] to the quantum regime has been the subject of extensive theoretical studies\n  2214→in the last decades [12–19]. As nano/micro technologies develop, there has been growing interest in\n  2215→experimentally testing the principles of quantum thermodynamics, which would be a solid foundation for\n  2216→further development of technologies in the quantum regime.\n  2217→Among diverse physical platforms, a trapped ion system is particularly interesting to test quantum\n  2218→thermodynamics [20–24]. One important feature in a quantum system, which distinguishes from classical\n  2219→macroscopic systems, is energy quantization. Historically, the finding of discretized energy levels of an\n  2220→atom has led to the birth of quantum mechanics [25] and the electron shelving technique can distinguish\n  2221→internal states of the trapped ion with near-perfect detection efficiency [26–30]. For the external degree\n  2222→of freedom of the atomic ion trapped in a harmonic potential, the technology of cooling to the ground\n  2223→state of the trap has been developed and the quantized energy levels of motion have been observed and\n  2224→manipulated through the resolved Raman laser technique [31–36]. The single trapped atomic ion can be\n  2225→considered as the most fundamental and the simplest system, which can be manipulated and measured\n  2226→with high precision enough to experimentally verify the principles of quantum thermodynamics.\n  2227→Generally, the principles of non-equilibrium thermodynamics are expressed as inequalities. For example,\n  2228→the work performed on a system in contact with a heat reservoir during an arbitrary drive of the system is\n  2229→lower bounded by the net change in its free energy: hW i ≥ ∆F , where the equality holds for equilibrium\n  2230→processes. When statistical fluctuations are properly incorporated in exponential form, the inequality is\n  2231→reformulated as equality, which is known as the Jarzynski equality [10],\n  2232→he−W/kB T i = e−∆F/kB T ,\n  2233→\n  2234→(1)\n  2235→\n  2236→\n  2237→=== 1904.04178v1.pdf ===\n  2238→Trapped-Ion Quantum Computing: Progress and Challenges\n  2239→Colin D. Bruzewicz,∗ John Chiaverini,† Robert McConnell,‡ and Jeremy M. Sage§\n  2240→\n  2241→arXiv:1904.04178v1 [quant-ph] 8 Apr 2019\n  2242→\n  2243→Lincoln Laboratory, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Lexington, MA, 02420\n  2244→(Dated: April 9, 2019)\n  2245→Trapped ions are among the most promising systems for practical quantum computing (QC). The\n  2246→basic requirements for universal QC have all been demonstrated with ions and quantum algorithms\n  2247→using few-ion-qubit systems have been implemented. We review the state of the field, covering the\n  2248→basics of how trapped ions are used for QC and their strengths and limitations as qubits. In addition,\n  2249→we discuss what is being done, and what may be required, to increase the scale of trapped ion\n  2250→quantum computers while mitigating decoherence and control errors. Finally, we explore the outlook\n  2251→for trapped-ion QC. In particular, we discuss near-term applications, considerations impacting the\n  2252→design of future systems of trapped ions, and experiments and demonstrations that may further\n  2253→inform these considerations.\n  2254→\n  2255→D. Quantum Control Demonstrations: Quantum\n  2256→Computing Algorithms and Primitives\n  2257→25\n  2258→\n  2259→CONTENTS\n  2260→\n  2261→I. Introduction\n  2262→A. Trapped Ions for Quantum Computing\n  2263→B. Scope of this Review\n  2264→C. DiVincenzo Criteria\n  2265→D. Pros and Cons of Trapped Ions as Qubits\n  2266→E. Considerations for Scaling a Trapped-Ion\n  2267→Quantum Computer\n  2268→\n  2269→2\n  2270→2\n  2271→2\n  2272→2\n  2273→3\n  2274→\n  2275→II. Trapped Ions as Qubits\n  2276→A. Trapping Individual Ions\n  2277→1. Types of Ion Traps\n  2278→2. Paul Traps for QC\n  2279→3. Miniature, Microfabricated, and\n  2280→Surface-Electrode Traps\n  2281→4. Loading Ions into Traps\n  2282→B. Internal States: Qubit Levels\n  2283→1. Zeeman Qubits\n  2284→2. Hyperfine Qubits\n  2285→3. Optical Qubits\n  2286→4. Fine-Structure Qubits\n  2287→C. Motional States\n  2288→\n  2289→=== 1904.10193v2.pdf ===\n  2290→Charged black holes in the Einstein-Maxwell-Weyl gravity\n  2291→Chao Wu1∗ , De-Cheng Zou1† , Ming Zhang2‡\n  2292→1\n  2293→\n  2294→Center for Gravitation and Cosmology and College of Physical Science and Technology,\n  2295→Yangzhou University, Yangzhou 225009, China\n  2296→2\n  2297→\n  2298→Faculty of Science, Xi’an Aeronautical University, Xi’an 710077 China\n  2299→\n  2300→arXiv:1904.10193v2 [gr-qc] 1 Oct 2019\n  2301→\n  2302→(Dated: October 2, 2019)\n  2303→\n  2304→We construct charged asymptotically flat black hole solutions in Einstein-MaxwellWeyl(EMW) gravity. These solutions can be interpreted as generalizations of two different\n  2305→groups: Schwarzschild black hole (SBH) and non-Schwarzschild black hole (NSBH) solutions.\n  2306→In addition, we discuss the thermodynamic properties of two groups of numerical solutions\n  2307→in detail, and show that they obey the first law of thermodynamics.\n  2308→PACS numbers: 04.70.Bw, 42.50.Nn, 04.30.Nk\n  2309→\n  2310→I.\n  2311→\n  2312→INTRODUCTION\n  2313→\n  2314→As well known, the general relativity can be viewed as an effective low-energy field theory in\n  2315→string theory. It’s also the first term in an infinite series of gravitational corrections built from\n  2316→powers of the curvature tensor and its derivatives [1]. In addition, the general relativity leaves\n  2317→some open fundamental questions, including the problems of singularity, non-renormalizability, the\n  2318→dark matter and dark energy. In order to answer these questions, Stelle et.al asserted one can\n  2319→add higher derivatives terms to the Einstein-Hilbert action [2]. In four-dimensional spacetime, the\n  2320→most general theory up to the second order in the curvature is given by [3–5]\n  2321→I=\n  2322→\n  2323→Z\n  2324→\n  2325→\u0003\n  2326→√ \u0002\n  2327→d4 x −g γR − αCµνρσ C µνρσ + βR2\n  2328→\n  2329→(1)\n  2330→\n  2331→where α, β and γ are constants, and Cµνρσ is the Weyl tensor. Notice that for any static, spherically\n  2332→symmetric black-hole solution, the term quadratic in Ricci scalar R makes no contribution to the\n  2333→corresponding field equations [6]. As a result, this setting of β = 0 and γ = 1 has been often\n  2334→applied for simplicity in the Einstein-Weyl (EW) gravity, and this theory reduces to the pure\n  2335→Einstein-Weyl gravity. By fixed α = 1/2, Lü et.al [4, 5] derived a new numerical non-Schwarzschild\n  2336→∗\n  2337→\n  2338→wc130330@163.com\n  2339→Corresponding author: dczou@yzu.edu.cn\n  2340→\n  2341→=== 1907.04292v1.pdf ===\n  2342→EVOLUTION OF THE INFORMATIONAL COMPLEXITY OF\n  2343→CONTEMPORARY WESTERN MUSIC\n  2344→Thomas Parmer, Yong-Yeol Ahn\n  2345→School of Informatics, Computing, and Engineering\n  2346→Indiana University, Bloomington, IN, USA\n  2347→tjparmer@indiana.edu, yyahn@iu.edu\n  2348→\n  2349→arXiv:1907.04292v1 [cs.SD] 9 Jul 2019\n  2350→\n  2351→ABSTRACT\n  2352→We measure the complexity of songs in the Million\n  2353→Song Dataset (MSD) in terms of pitch, timbre, loudness,\n  2354→and rhythm to investigate their evolution from 1960 to\n  2355→2010. By comparing the Billboard Hot 100 with random\n  2356→samples, we find that the complexity of popular songs\n  2357→tends to be more narrowly distributed around the mean,\n  2358→supporting the idea of an inverted U-shaped relationship\n  2359→between complexity and hedonistic value. We then examine the temporal evolution of complexity, reporting consistent changes across decades, such as a decrease in average loudness complexity since the 1960s, and an increase\n  2360→in timbre complexity overall but not for popular songs.\n  2361→We also show, in contrast to claims that popular songs\n  2362→sound more alike over time, that they are not more similar\n  2363→than they were 50 years ago in terms of pitch or rhythm,\n  2364→although similarity in timbre shows distinctive patterns\n  2365→across eras and similarity in loudness has been increasing.\n  2366→Finally, we show that musical genres can be differentiated\n  2367→by their distinctive complexity profiles.\n  2368→1. INTRODUCTION\n  2369→Our everyday life is surrounded by cultural products; we\n  2370→wake up to a song, read a book on the subway, watch a\n  2371→movie with friends, or even travel far to admire a piece\n  2372→of art. Despite such pervasiveness, we cannot fully explain why we like a particular song over others or what\n  2373→makes something a great piece of art. Although the perceived quality of a piece is affected by numerous contextual factors, including one’s cultural, social, and emotional\n  2374→background, theories suggest that preference, or ‘hedonistic value’, may also be affected by innate properties of the\n  2375→products, such as novelty and complexity [1, 18, 28]. In\n  2376→particular, a popular theory suggests there is a Goldilocks\n  2377→principle — that just the right amount of novelty or complexity elicits the largest amount of pleasure, whereas\n  2378→pieces with too little or too much complexity are less interesting and enjoyable [3]. On the other hand, cultural\n  2379→c Thomas Parmer, Yong-Yeol Ahn. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0). Attribution: Thomas Parmer, Yong-Yeol Ahn. “Evolution of the Informational Complexity of Contemporary Western Music”, 20th International\n  2380→Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference, Delft, The Netherlands, 2019.\n  2381→\n  2382→products are also fashionable — what is popular now may\n  2383→be completely out of fashion next month. Such seemingly\n  2384→contrasting observations prompt us to ask the following\n  2385→questions: as fads come and go, is there still a consistent\n  2386→preference towards the optimal amount of complexity in\n  2387→cultural products? How has the complexity of contemporary cultural products changed over time?\n  2388→This question may apply to any type of cultural product, but we focus here on the complexity of contemporary\n  2389→Western songs. Although various studies have already reported evidence of the ‘inverted U-shaped’ relationship between perceived complexity and the pleasantness of music in terms of individual-level preference [1, 11, 32], evidence of this preference at the population-level is unclear [7, 22, 30], and many past studies have been limited\n  2390→by the size or extent of the data, in terms of genres or temporal range.\n  2391→Recently, datasets such as the Million Song Dataset\n  2392→\n  2393→=== 1908.10275v1.pdf ===\n  2394→The Wiki Music dataset:\n  2395→A tool for computational analysis of popular music\n  2396→Fabio Celli\n  2397→\n  2398→arXiv:1908.10275v1 [cs.CL] 27 Aug 2019\n  2399→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"ad0bda88-90da-4d29-b8e5-302e75f756f1","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:03:43.737Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"df8caf54-0fa0-4bed-a3a5-140e57363b07"}
